(window.__CLAIMS_ADD__||function(){})([{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F1: FDC 175085 roasted deer, per 100 g cooked: 158 kcal, 30.2 g protein, 3.19 g fat, 1.25 g sat fat, 112 mg cholesterol. Per 3 oz by arithmetic: 134 kcal, 25.7 g protein, 2.71 g fat, 1.06 g sat. Note: the entry names no cut, and its fat value carries a 1989 date with dataPoints 0.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a1"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U33: FDC 172604, deer loin, lean only, broiled, per 100 g: 150 kcal, 30.2 g protein, 2.38 g fat (n=6, analytical, range 1.6 to 3.4), 1.35 g sat, 79 mg cholesterol. The per-3-oz figures (128 kcal, 2.02 g fat, 1.15 g sat, 67.2 mg cholesterol) are arithmetic and check out.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a2"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U34: FDC 168634, beef top sirloin, lean only, trimmed to 0\" fat, broiled, per 100 g: 183 kcal, 30.6 g protein, 5.79 g fat, 82 mg cholesterol. Sat fat at FDC is 2.2 g, not 2.21 as the mirror had; the difference is trivial and per 3 oz is still 1.87 g. Per 3 oz: 156 kcal, 4.92 g fat.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a3"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U76: chicken breast, meat only, roasted (171477), per 100 g: 165 kcal, 31.0 g protein, 3.57 g fat, 1.01 g sat, 85 mg cholesterol. Pork tenderloin, lean only, roasted (168250): 143 kcal, 26.2 g protein, 3.51 g fat, 1.2 g sat, 73 mg cholesterol. Per 3 oz (arithmetic): chicken 140 kcal, 3.0 g fat; pork 122 kcal, 2.98 g fat, 62 mg cholesterol.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a4"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F1 headline: lean cooked venison is about as lean as skinless chicken breast and pork tenderloin, and leaner than lean sirloin. Other named deer cuts back this up (per 100 g cooked): top round 1.92 g fat, 152 kcal; tenderloin 2.35 g fat, 149 kcal. Pork tenderloin has fewer calories than every deer cut, so venison is not uniquely lean.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a5"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U46, raw like-for-like. Wrong in the claim: chicken breast raw \"about 2.4 g fat / 22.4 g protein\" and bison top sirloin raw \"about 1.8 g\". At FDC, chicken breast raw (171077) is 2.62 g fat, 22.5 g protein, 120 kcal, and bison top sirloin lean raw (172564) is 2.4 g fat, 113 kcal. Whole chicken meat-only raw (171052) is 3.08 g, as claimed. Raw deer (173855) is 2.42 g fat, 23.0 g protein, 120 kcal. So raw venison is marginally leaner than raw chicken breast (0.2 g), a tie in practice, and bison is level with deer, not leaner. Source of the error: the finder's mirror read / approximation.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-a6"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U47: cooked, deer and chicken breast are level on fat (3.19 vs 3.57 g/100 g, a 0.38 g gap) and deer is higher on cholesterol (112 vs 85 mg). Deer beats chicken on iron (4.47 vs 1.04 mg). On B12 the claim leans on the shaky raw 6.31 entry (see A16). Use the analytically measured cooked loin instead, 1.83 vs 0.34 ug for roasted chicken: still about 5x.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a7"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F2: \"venison is lower in cholesterol\" is not supported. Deer rows per 100 g: raw 85 mg (n=43, the best-sampled deer value; the dossier left it out), loin cooked 79, top round 85, tenderloin 88, ground cooked 98, roasted 112, shoulder braised 113. Comparators: chicken breast raw 73 / roasted 85; 80/20 beef raw 71; 85/15 raw 68; sirloin cooked 82; pork tenderloin cooked 73. Venison's cholesterol is the same as or higher than beef and chicken.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a8"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F3 rows: raw ground deer (172602) 7.13 g fat, 3.36 g sat, 157 kcal; 80/20 beef raw (174036) 20.0 g fat, 7.58 g sat, 254 kcal; ground deer pan-broiled (172603) 8.22 g fat; 85/15 patty pan-broiled (174033) 14.0 g. FDC also has 90/10 raw beef (174030) at 10.0 g fat, 176 kcal, which the dossier said was never fetched.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a9"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U35: 85/15 patty pan-broiled (174033), per 100 g: 232 kcal, 24.6 g protein, 14.0 g fat, 5.33 g sat, 84 mg cholesterol. Per 85 g: 197 kcal, 11.9 g fat. Cooked ground deer (172603) per 85 g: 159 kcal, 22.4 g protein, 7.0 g fat, 3.39 g sat, 83 mg cholesterol. All per-85 g figures are arithmetic.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a10"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U81: FNDDS \"Beef, ground, raw\" (2705853) is 14.88 g fat, 5.67 g sat, 213 kcal per 100 g, vs ground deer raw 7.13 / 3.36 / 157, so \"about half the fat\" holds for these generic entries only. Caveat as claimed: the USDA ground-deer entry does not say whether it has added fat.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a11"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U41 / F3 \"processors commonly add 10-20% fat for burger and 20-30% for sausage\". Wrong: the source is one MeatEater writer's rule of thumb for grinding at home (\"My rule of thumb\"), not a statement about what processors add, and it is no survey. Right: the guide says 10-20% for burger and 20% minimum / 30% standard for sausage, with fatback preferred. The arithmetic also assumed \"pure\" fat; FDC pork backfat is 88.7 g fat/100 g and beef suet 94.0 g. Redone: 90% lean deer (2.42 g) + 10% fatback gives about 11% fat; 80% + 20% gives about 20%. That is still the 90/10 to 80/20 beef range, so the conclusion stands. Source of the error: engine/finder wording (\"Processors add\").","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-a12"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F4 iron: lean venison clearly beats beef and chicken. Cooked deer cuts run 4.09 to 5.01 mg/100 g, against 85/15 patty 2.60 (broiled) or 2.68 (pan-broiled), sirloin 1.96, chicken breast 1.04 and pork tenderloin 1.15. Per 3 oz (arithmetic): deer loin 3.5 mg (19% DV), sirloin 1.7 mg (9%), chicken 0.9 mg (5%).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a13"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F4 \"beef beats venison on zinc by more than 2x (6.31 vs 2.75 mg)\". Wrong: that ratio holds only for the generic roasted deer entry against a broiled 85/15 patty. Right: the named cooked deer cuts carry 3.63 (loin), 3.67 (top round), 3.99 (tenderloin), 5.2 (ground) and 8.64 mg (shoulder clod, braised), against 5.71 for lean sirloin and 6.2 to 6.31 for 85/15 patties. Beef is usually higher in zinc than lean venison steaks, by about 1.2 to 1.6x, and braised venison shoulder beats beef. Source of the error: the engine picked one entry pair.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-a14"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U37: raw deer (173855) figures confirmed: iron 3.4, zinc 2.09, B12 6.31, niacin 6.37 per 100 g; the per-3-oz DV percentages are correct arithmetic.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a15"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U37's conclusion, \"the one big win is B12 (about 220% DV)\". Wrong: the 6.31 ug figure in raw whole deer has dataPoints 0 and no derivation code in FDC. Right: every deer entry where B12 was measured (Analytical, n=3) gives 1.83 to 3.62 ug/100 g cooked: loin 1.83, top round 2.27, ground 2.32, shoulder 3.06, tenderloin 3.62. That is the same range as beef (sirloin 1.71, 85/15 patty 2.64-2.79). Per 3 oz cooked loin = 1.56 ug, about 65% DV (arithmetic). NZ's industry figure of 1.3 ug (A32) agrees. Venison has a lot of B12, like beef, not 2x beef. Source of the error: the finder trusted one undocumented entry; the engine flagged it as \"unreliable\" but never checked the other cuts.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-a16"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U38: raw ground deer (172602) iron 2.92, zinc 4.2, B12 1.87, niacin 5.7, protein 21.8, fat 7.13. Raw 85/15 beef (171796): iron 2.09, zinc 4.48, B12 2.17, niacin 4.65. Per 3 oz DV (arithmetic): deer 14/32/66/30%, beef 10/35/77/25%, as claimed. Ground venison's micronutrient edge is mostly gone apart from iron.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a17"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U38 (cooked): roasted deer (175085) iron 4.47, zinc 2.75, niacin 6.71, no B12 row; broiled 85/15 patty (174032) iron 2.6, zinc 6.31, B12 2.64, niacin 5.38. The DV percentages are correct arithmetic. The zinc generalisation is corrected in A14.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a18"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U53: raw chicken breast (171077) iron 0.37, zinc 0.68, B12 0.21, niacin 9.6. Chicken beats venison only on niacin (raw), as claimed; cooked, chicken still wins (roasted breast 13.7 mg vs deer loin 10.8).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a19"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U54: FDA Daily Values iron 18 mg, zinc 11 mg, B12 2.4 mcg, niacin 16 mg NE (also sodium 2,300 mg, saturated fat 20 g, cholesterol 300 mg). None of the FDC entries saved here carries a heme-iron nutrient (no saved entry carries one), so only total iron is comparable.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a20"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F5 (verifier-confirmed, wording from an abstract; sampled): Cordain et al. 2002 measured muscle n-6/n-3 of 2.32 to 2.60 in 17 elk, 15 mule deer and 17 antelope (no whitetails). The comparison with cattle is a LITERATURE comparison, not beef measured in the paper. PubMed tags it as a Review.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a21"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U39: the Purdue release (EurekAlert, 4 Feb 2002) gives grain-fed steers 5:1 to 13:1 \"reported in previous studies\", against slightly above 2:1 for wild ruminants and grass-fed beef. Note for the slogan check: the same release says these fats \"lower cholesterol\", a press-release claim the paper did not test.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a22"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F5 \"similar to pasture-fed cattle, better than grain-fed\". FDC itself backs this. Grass-fed strip steak, lean, raw (169429): 2.69 g fat, 117 kcal; linoleic n-6 0.041 g against n-3 (ALA 0.015 + EPA 0.002 + DPA 0.004) = about 2:1. Conventional top loin, lean, raw, all grades (174002): 5.67 g fat, 143 kcal; 0.247 / (0.009 + 0.002 + 0.008) = about 13:1. Ratios are my arithmetic from FDC rows. Lean grass-fed beef is as lean as venison (2.69 vs 2.42 g raw).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a23"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U40 / F5 \"USDA raw deer: EPA 0, DHA 0\". Wrong: these zeros are not measurements. Raw deer (173855) has dataPoints 0 and no derivation; the loin, ground and tenderloin entries are labelled \"Assumed zero\". Right: studies that measured deer muscle find EPA and DHA (fallow deer EPA 1.19-1.40% and DHA 0.72-0.82% of fatty acids, A25). At about 2.4 g fat per 100 g that is roughly 0.03 g EPA per 100 g (my arithmetic, order of magnitude only). Wild Atlantic salmon raw (173686) has EPA 0.321 + DHA 1.12 = 1.44 g. So \"essentially none next to salmon\" holds; \"zero\" does not. ALA 0.07 g (18:3) confirmed. Source of the error: finder read the mirror's zeros as data.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-a24"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U55: 45 farmed male fallow deer (Czech Republic), longissimus n-6/n-3 2.38 (pasture) / 2.63 (barley) / 2.67 (barley + lysine); pooled mean 2.56, \"well below\" the guideline of under five. EPA and DHA higher in the pasture-only group.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a25"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U106: the ICoMST-style paper (Purchas et al., NZ pasture-finished red deer stags and hinds) found higher EPA and DHA than pasture-finished beef, allowing for fat differences, but \"low compared to levels in oily fish\". The paper is confirmed. The absence half (\"no peer-reviewed whitetail EPA/DHA measurement\") was not disproved by this pass, but absence can't be confirmed from one search.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a26"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U107: Lithuanian wild vs farmed red deer (Zoology and Ecology 27(2), 2017). The paper exists (Crossref: Bliznikas et al., pp. 176-183), but the abstract could not be read at a primary: the LSMU CRIS page is a JavaScript shell, T&F returned 403, and Crossref has no abstract. The \"free-living ratio more favourable\" finding is unverified. Don't air it.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-a27"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U21 / F6: IARC defines red meat as all mammalian muscle meat and lists beef, veal, pork, lamb, mutton, horse, goat; it does not name venison or game. The 2A call rests on \"limited\" epidemiological evidence plus strong mechanistic evidence, with 17% higher colorectal risk per 100 g/day IF causal. Venison falls under \"all mammalian muscle meat\", so IARC neither exempts nor names it.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a28"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F6: no venison-specific health-outcome study. A PubMed search (venison OR \"deer meat\" in title/abstract AND cardiovascular/cholesterol/lipid/cohort/trial/mortality/cancer/health) returned 110 records. By title, none is a human cohort or trial of venison eating and heart or cancer outcomes; they cover composition, pathogens, lead, PFAS, CWD. \"Healthier than beef\" is a nutrient-profile inference. This verdict is bounded by that one search.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a29"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U77 / F7: FNDDS venison/deer jerky (2705915), per 100 g: 398 kcal, 32.2 g protein, 22.41 g fat, 2,955 mg sodium (14 g strip = 414 mg, 18% DV, arithmetic). Beef jerky (2705860): 1,785 mg/100 g (18 g = 321 mg). Venison sausage (2705916): 312 kcal, 20.23 g protein, 829 mg sodium, 25.08 g fat/100 g (35 g patty = 8.8 g fat). Raw deer sodium 51 mg (n=19). These are FNDDS \"Survey\" recipe entries, modelled rather than lab samples. Note: the sausage's 25 g fat/100 g beats 80/20 raw beef (20 g).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a30"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F3/F7 serving size the viewer can picture: FDC's ground-deer entry defines its serving as one patty cooked from 4 oz raw = 85 g, i.e. a cooked quarter-pound patty is the 3-oz serving used in every per-3-oz figure here.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a31"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U84: NZ Venison (industry body) lists cooked farmed medallion per 100 g: 1.8 g fat, 0.8 g sat, 28 g protein, 4.8 mg iron, 1.3 ug B12. Comparators: beef scotch fillet 13.2 g fat, lamb loin 4.8, chicken breast (no skin) 5.2. Cites the NZ Food Composition Database 2018. Marketing, and farmed red deer, not wild whitetail.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a32"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U87: Deer & Deer Hunting (Daniel Schmidt, 25 June 2021) calls venison \"the healthiest red meat on the planet\" and tabulates whitetail 2.2 g fat vs \"extra-lean ground beef\" 18.5 g vs skinless grilled chicken 5.7 g per 4 oz, with no source. Extra: its own table contradicts its text. It says axis venison is \"lower in cholesterol than all of those other meats\", while its chicken row (55 mg) is lower than both axis (59) and whitetail (70). USDA cooked deer vs chicken breast fat gap = 0.38 g/100 g (A7).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a33"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F27: \"healthiest red meat\" is unsupported. On FDC numbers, lean venison ties chicken breast and pork tenderloin on fat; lean grass-fed beef (A23) and bison (A6) are as lean; cholesterol is no lower (A8); zinc usually trails beef (A14). The real edge is iron (A13). \"Among the leanest meats\" holds.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a34"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U85 (part of F27; the CWD science is B2's): Wild beim Wild headlines \"Zombie deer disease CWD: new study shows transmission risk to humans\", yet its own text says the study \"fails to provide conclusive proof\" and that the hunter cases are not conclusively confirmed.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a35"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U70, beef supply for scale. Wrong in the claim: \"Total 2024 beef production was 27,789 million lb\"; 27,789 is the PORK row. Right: ERS LDP-M-373 (17 July 2025) gives 2024 beef production of 26,984 million lb (2025 forecast 26,188). Per-capita beef disappearance of 59.1 retail lb in 2024 (and 59.1 forecast for 2025, 58.4 for 2026) is confirmed. The \"28.7 billion lb disappearance / 20.1 billion retail\" figure was not found on the ERS Cattle & Beef sector page fetched here, so don't air it. Source of the error: finder misread the table row.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-a36"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F2 / F4 \"venison is lower in fat AND an iron source like beef\": for burger the edge narrows but holds only for unblended meat. Per 3 oz cooked (arithmetic): ground deer 7.0 g fat, 85/15 patty 11.9 g, 80/20 patty 15.1 g; iron 2.85 vs 2.28 vs 2.11 mg. With 20% fatback added (A12), the deer burger is in 80/20 territory.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a37"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F8: as of June 2026, CWD is documented in 37 US states and five Canadian provinces, wild and captive herds counted together (USGS ver. 4.0, August 2026).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b1-1"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F8: \"CDC also says 37\". WRONG: the CDC page (dated Jan. 21, 2026, before the USGS June update) says wild deer in 36 states. The error came from the engine finding (F8). Air the USGS 37 and attribute it to USGS, not the CDC.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-b1-2"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F8: a count of the USGS wild-only CSV also gives 37 states, and Michigan's 18 rows match the DNR's \"18 counties\". My script count: 927 rows, 37 states in the wild file. The captive file has 22 states, none of them captive-only. Michigan has 18 wild rows with the same names the DNR lists. Newaygo, one of the five peg counties, is not among them.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b1-3"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U57: on the June 2026 update, ND, WY, ID, MT and CO moved from counties to wildlife-management units, so county counts are not comparable. My count: 181 rows are units only, and a further 165 Colorado rows carry both a county and a unit (CO 165, WY 112, MT 49, ND 15, ID 5). Never air a national county count.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b1-4"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F8/U78 timeline: first noted in 1967 in research mule deer in Colorado, first in wild elk in Colorado in 1981, white-tailed deer in South Dakota wild herds in 2001, Wisconsin's first positives in 2002.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b1-5"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F8/U78: \"confined to CO/WY until ~2000\". Only true of the WILD endemic zone. By 2000 it was in captive herds in Saskatchewan (mid-1990s), Oklahoma and Nebraska, and in wild cervids in Saskatchewan. Script wording that holds: \"for decades a Colorado-Wyoming problem in the wild\". The TAMU page is old (it says 21 states), so use it only for the early dates.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b1-6"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"For the \"how the map changed\" picture, USGS ships official year-by-year maps: one for \"prior to 2000\", then yearly 2004-2025. That is the sourced basis for a before/after map.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b1-7"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F9: Wyoming 2025: 21.6% of hunter-harvested ADULT MALE mule deer and 32.1% of adult male whitetails (males only, never \"one in five Wyoming deer\"). Adult elk 2.4%.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b1-8"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F9: CWD is in 35 of 37 Wyoming mule deer herds. The Project herd's five-year (2021-2025) average is 61.9%, 95% CI 31.6-68.9%.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b1-9"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F9: Wisconsin CWD-year 2025 (Apr 2025-Mar 2026), all submitted deer. My arithmetic on the DNR table: Richland 428/1,198 = 35.7%, Sauk 288/872 = 33.0%, Lafayette 49/158 = 31.0%, Columbia 169/616 = 27.4%, Iowa 170/724 = 23.5%, Dane 193/1,002 = 19.3%. Summed over 73 rows: 2,041 positive of 18,370. These are submissions, not random samples, and the DNR warns against reading them as county prevalence.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b1-10"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U75: Saskatchewan 2025-26: 2,900 submissions (up from 2,248), 362 positive (195 whitetails, 125 mule deer, 33 elk, 9 moose), mandatory testing in WMZs 43, 47, 50 and 55, CWD in 66 of 83 zones.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b1-11"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"NEW, for the peg: in the CWD year before the detection, Marinette County submitted 183 deer and none tested positive. (The table row is quoted with its pipes escaped.)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b1-12"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"PEG, Wisconsin: on 2026-10-22 the DNR confirmed the first CWD case in a wild deer in Marinette County, near Peshtigo. It was a 3-year-old buck, put down because it showed signs of illness.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b1-13"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"PEG, Wisconsin consequence (new): the detection extends Marinette's feeding and baiting ban by three years.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b1-14"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"PEG, Michigan, 2026-10-12: the DNR asks hunters in Mecosta, Newaygo, Kent, Montcalm and Ionia counties to submit deer heads, with 24/7 drop boxes open through Jan. 4. Why now: the area's last intensive sampling was in 2019.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b1-15"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"PEG, Michigan: the release says CWD is known in 18 Michigan counties and 37 US states, has not been shown to be transmissible to humans, and that an infected deer can look and behave normally.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b1-16"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"PEG, Minnesota, 2026-10-26: more than 400,000 firearms hunters are expected for the Nov. 7 opener, and a four-year statewide rotational CWD plan begins with sampling required in the southwest on Nov. 7-8. The 10-26 release IS now read as a document, which closes the dossier's gap.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b1-17"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U30: rotation order is SW 2026, NW 2027, NE 2028, SE 2029, for every deer one year or older on opening weekend. It can be met at staffed or self-service stations, with mail-in kits, or at Partner Sampling Program taxidermists and processors.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b1-18"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"NEW, Minnesota's reason for the change: two-thirds of the state has had no targeted look in 20+ years, and there are detections outside the zones. A recent positive wild deer was found near Stillwater (DPA 236).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b1-19"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"PEG openers: Minnesota Nov. 7, Michigan regular firearm season Nov. 15-30, Wisconsin gun season Nov. 21-29, 2026.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b1-20"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U43: Wisconsin 2025 season: 17,759 sampled, 2,006 positive, average 8.9 days from drop-off to emailed result, 242 sites (163 kiosks, 46 staffed). This is a news report citing the DNR. The DNR's CWD-year table (B1-10) gives 18,370/2,041 because it covers a different window, so do not mix the two.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b1-21"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U43/F17 uptake: 339,481 deer registered in Wisconsin in all seasons (preliminary, as of Jan. 20). 17,759 / 339,481 is about 5.2%: my arithmetic, so about 1 in 19 deer tested. Testing is free.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b1-22"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F17: the Wisconsin turnaround is stated three ways: an 8.9-day average (2025, B1-21), \"10-14 days\" in an average year (DNR sampling page) and \"within two weeks\" (2021 release). Air \"one to two weeks\". Note: U104 attributes the 10-14 days to the DNR lead page; it is on the sampling page, not the lead page.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b1-23"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U79: Minnesota 2025: about 14,000 samples, \"a little more than 7%\" of 186,203 deer harvested, 117 positive. The kit and test are free, and partners may charge for their service. TRAP: the same article calls 117 \"a little more than 8%\" of samples. 117/14,000 is 0.8%, so never air \"8%\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b1-24"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"NEW, Minnesota turnaround: screening takes about 3 days, confirmation up to a week more, and results typically arrive in 7-14 days.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b1-25"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"NEW, the key answer to \"is there time to eat before results?\": Minnesota DNR says eating before results is the hunter's choice and calls the test not a food-safety test. Wisconsin's health department says the opposite for CWD areas: don't eat or share it until the result says not detected (B1-32).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b1-26"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F17 Michigan cost, which the dossier had left unverified: 2026 testing is free in the five focus counties through DNR drop boxes and staffed sites (heads only, no kits there). Elsewhere a free lymph-node kit with overnight shipping goes to MSU's lab, and fee labs are an option.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b1-27"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F17 Michigan turnaround: no Michigan page read here (the 10-12 release, the testing page, the DeerCheck page, the 2026 regs summary) states a turnaround time. Do not air a Michigan number.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-b1-28"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F16/U97: Minnesota DNR says most CWD-positive harvested deer show no outward signs.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b1-29"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F16/U97: \"Wisconsin DHS says infected deer typically look healthy for many months\". The DHS page could not be reached (five dhs.wisconsin.gov URLs returned 404). A DNR release relaying DHS says infected deer \"can look healthy\", with no \"many months\". Air \"can look healthy\" and attribute it to Wisconsin's health department via the DNR.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b1-30"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F16 support from Michigan: lab testing is the only way to tell. Irony for the script: the Marinette deer that made the news was the exception, killed BECAUSE it looked sick (B1-13).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b1-31"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U90/U97: the Wisconsin DNR health advisory says cooking does not deactivate CWD prions and that prions can be in the muscle of late-stage deer. Wisconsin's health department (DHS) advises not eating or giving away venison from CWD-affected areas until the result is \"not detected\". The \"2023 DNR release\" saying DHS, CDC and WHO all advise against eating positive deer was not opened. A 2022 DNR release says the same thing.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b1-32"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"CDC field advice, cited by F16 (the human-risk science is B2's): don't shoot, handle or eat sick-looking animals, strongly consider testing, and don't eat a positive animal.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b1-33"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U71: the Wisconsin Deer Donation Program has taken over 100,000 deer and turned them into over four million pounds of ground venison since 2000. Donating is free, the public can add $1 or more, CWD testing follows three county tiers, the processor may hold meat until results, and some sites refuse deer that need testing.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b1-34"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"NEW trap: as fetched on 2026-11-01, the donation page still lists Marinette in the first tier, \"No CWD Testing Required\" (the three lists run in that order: Ashland ... Marinette ... Washburn, then Chippewa ... Wood, then Adams ... Waukesha). That is ten days after the county's first positive. Do not tell viewers Marinette donors must test, and do not call the page wrong either: it may simply not be updated yet.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b1-35"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U72: Hunters Feeding Michigan requires CWD testing for 18 named counties, costs donors nothing, takes only legal, harvest-reported, whole deer, refuses deer from within 3 miles of Clark's Marsh (PFAS \"Do Not Eat\"), and does not list Newaygo. WRONG DETAIL: the TB list has 16 counties, but two of them (Mecosta, Ogemaw) are also on the CWD list, so they are not \"16 other counties\". The error came from the finder's wording.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-b1-36"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U73/F17: \"DATCP lets processors combine ONLY deer with 'not detected' results\". WRONG: the 2021 release presents it as a choice processors MAY make (batching only not-detected deer, so they can promise customers clean trim), not as a limit on what they may combine. Right: Wisconsin hunters are told to ASK whether their sausage and jerky contain trim from untested deer. The \"no hold-until-result rule\" is an absence I could not prove either way. The error came from the finder's paraphrase.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-b1-37"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U98: Minnesota replaced carcass-movement limits in CWD management zones with a disposal rule (refuse hauler, landfill, meat processor, or on-site on private land with permission). Before, hunters had to quarter the deer or wait for a not-detected result. Quartering still applies to deer brought in from out of state. The rule is silent on holding meat: \"it doesn't require holding meat\" is our inference, not the rule's words.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b1-38"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U99/F17: Minnesota agriculture (MDA) guidance tells processors to hold the carcass or trim until results arrive (or debone at end of day and hold the trim), and to process a positive deer the hunter keeps on its own. WRONG DATE: the dossier calls it a 2023 bulletin, but the page fetched today describes the 2026 four-year rotation, so it is current. It also says processors MAY take untested deer. It is guidance, not statute. The error came from the finder.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-b1-39"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F17 framing, \"testing is free or cheap in the peg states but few deer are tested\": free state testing in all three (WI statewide, MN statewide, MI in the focus counties plus free kits). Measured shares are about 5% of Wisconsin's harvest and about 7.5% of Minnesota's (B1-22, B1-24).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b1-40"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"Light detail: a Wisconsin hunter can bring a refrigerated head in up to five days after harvest, and a buck hunter who wants only the antlers can skull-cap before sampling.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b1-41"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F10: the 2026 macaque study inoculated 18 female cynomolgus macaques with elk, mule deer and white-tailed deer CWD material, plus 3 mock controls given CWD-negative cervid material.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b2-1"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F10: seven early-euthanized macaques were analysed, up to 7.5 years. By the animal IDs named in the text, the seven are 3 oral (AU501, AU467, AU316) and 4 intracerebral (AU408, AU469, plus AU519 and AU389 by steel-wire implant); that is my count from the named animals, and table S1 was not read. The paper is internally inconsistent: Results say seven, Methods say eight. The time window is given as 4-10, ~4.5-7.5 and 5-10 years, and most animals examined in detail were at 5-6 years. Say \"seven were analysed, mostly 5-6 years after exposure\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b2-2"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F10: \"two macaques had non-diagnostic clinical signs\". The AUTHORS call the signs \"indicative of prion disease\" (one oral, AU501; one intracerebral, AU389). \"Not diagnostic\" is the view of the outside expert Andreoletti, not the authors. Wasting was seen in five, three possibly from diabetes. Attribute each view to whoever holds it.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b2-3"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F10: the standard tests (immunoblot for protease-resistant PrPSc) found nothing in any macaque CNS, and IHC was inconclusive.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b2-4"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F10: PMCA was positive in 4/4 brains first tested; on serial dilution in 6/7 brains and 4/6 spleens; controls negative. Detail not in the dossier: the PMCA substrate was brain from mice expressing HUMAN prion protein, so macaque tissue seeded misfolding of human-sequence PrP in a test tube. The signal was weak: positive only to a 10^-5 dilution, against 10^-10 in terminal disease.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b2-5"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F10: RT-QuIC was positive in some spinal cord and brainstem regions. Precisely: only two macaques were run (AU501 oral, AU519 intracerebral). The positives were pons and cervical cord in AU501 and thoracic/lumbar cord in AU519.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b2-6"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F10 script trap says \"the paper conflicts with itself on TgElk (20%, 1 of 5, vs tissue counts 4/7)\". WRONG: there is no conflict. \"Four of the seven\" means four of the SEVEN POSITIVE MICE had received spinal-cord inocula (2 spleen, 1 brain); it is not a 4/7 attack rate. Attack rates were up to 20%, and no mouse showed PrPSc on immunoblot. Error source: engine finding (misread table S3 summary).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-b2-7"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F10: \"100% on second passage in bank voles and TgDeer mice\". The real chain: macaque AU519 (intracerebral, steel wire) → TgElk mouse → second TgElk passage → bank voles (100%) → voles again (100%) → TgDeer mice (100%). The 100% figures are rodents several passages removed from ONE intracerebrally implanted monkey, not the orally fed ones. Oral macaque AU501's tissue gave only low-level seeding in TgElk mice. Error source: engine finding, compressed.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-b2-8"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F10/U88: the authors' own conclusion is that the results challenge views that minimise risk, and do NOT imply efficient transmission.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b2-9"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F10/U88: the authors' caveats: animals probably preclinical, residual inoculum can't be excluded, isolates not strain-typed; they also raise lab contamination as an alternative to test (and argue against it).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b2-10"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F11/U88: why the authors think they differ from the NIH (RML) result: female-only macaques of about 4 years, diverse isolates including muscle, higher doses.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b2-11"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U48: outside experts dispute how much the paper shows (CIDRAP, published 2026-07-30). Andreoletti (Toulouse): the species barrier \"may not be absolute\", but transmission is neither efficient nor classical, and residual inoculum is a serious possibility for the wire-implanted animals. Bartz (Creighton): the prions look newly generated, and the key question is replication.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b2-12"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F12 script trap says \"no agency response to the paper was found\". WRONG: CDC's Ryan Maddox commented in the CIDRAP piece of 2026-07-30, after the paper. CDC has found no evidence of CWD in people and says more years of vigilance are needed. (The CDC web page itself, updated 2026-01-21, still predates the paper.) Error source: engine finding (search gap).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-b2-13"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F11/U56: Race 2014 had 6 intracerebral and 8 oral macaques with no disease at 124 months, and squirrel monkeys were susceptible (100% intracerebral, 92% oral). Precisely: 9 were dosed orally and one was removed at 1 month (colonic torsion), leaving 0/8. \"124\" is the current month count for the surviving animals; the animals screened so far were examined at 49-106 months. The doses in U56 match the table.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b2-14"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F11/U56: Race 2018 had seven CWD-inoculated macaques euthanized 11-13 years after inoculation, plus 8 uninoculated controls, screened by RT-QuIC, IHC and immunoblot: no evidence of transmission.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b2-15"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F11/U56: Czub's preliminary (2017) results \"alarmed the press\" and became the more cautious 2026 paper. Primary found: Health Canada's internal Risk Advisory Opinion (26 Apr 2017) described the unpublished CFIA-led results as TRANSMISSION, including oral feeding of muscle. It also said they did not change its food advice. The 2026 peer-reviewed wording is \"limited transmission\" with low-level seeding.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b2-16"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U56: the 2017 press numbers (3 of 5 macaques fed 5 kg of meat over 3 years positive; 2 of 3 with symptoms). Not in any primary I saved. Press accounts differ (search snippets also give \"5 of 18\"). Don't air them.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-b2-17"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"Transgenic-mouse and organoid context, as the papers describe it. Mice expressing human PrP were not susceptible in the studies Race 2014 cites. The 2026 authors themselves concede that numerous rodent studies point to low risk. The 2024 NIH (Haigh) human brain-organoid study found no CWD propagation at 180 days (via CIDRAP News; the paper itself was not opened).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b2-18"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F12/U89: CDC advice: strongly consider testing; never eat meat from a positive animal; don't shoot, handle or eat sick-acting animals or touch animals found dead; wear gloves. Page updated 2026-01-21 (before the 27 May 2026 paper).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b2-19"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U23/F12: CDC says no human infection has been reported, it is unknown whether people can be infected, and \"monkey studies are why it advises testing\". The first two are verbatim. The third is an inference: the page ties monkey studies to CWD being \"in theory\" a risk and gives the testing advice separately. Cross-domain note for B1: this page says wild deer in 36 states, not 37 (F8 says \"CDC also says 37\").","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b2-20"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U59: CIDRAP's 2025 report says CDC supports enhanced CJD surveillance in eight states and hunter studies in CO, WI and WY. It says US surveillance may not be sensitive enough to catch early spillover, human incubation is likely 10+ years, and the attack rate may be low (178 vCJD cases among ~5 million UK residents who ate BSE beef). These lines are CIDRAP's, not the CDC page the dossier cites.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b2-21"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U74/F12: Canada: no confirmed human cases and no direct evidence of transmission; don't eat any part of a positive animal; voluntary testing exists. The source is an Agriculture and Agri-Food Canada Question Period note (received 11 Dec 2025) stating Health Canada's position.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b2-22"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U74/F12: Alberta's page citing a WHO 1997 call to keep prion-infected animals out of the food chain. Neither the Alberta page nor WHO 1997 was fetched. The nearest primary: Health Canada (2017) says its position has matched WHO's \"since the late 1990s\" and quotes WHO's 2012 wording. Say \"the WHO has long advised…\", not \"WHO 1997 said…\".","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-b2-23"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U100/F12: EFSA 2019 (CWD III): the risk from eating infected meat cannot be directly assessed; consumers WILL be exposed; data allow no conclusion on zoonotic potential. \"Only excluding whole carcasses eliminates exposure\" is EFSA's 2017 Part II conclusion, restated in the 2019 opinion. The 2019 opinion itself says testing and removal of positive carcasses or high-risk tissues \"would reduce\" exposure.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b2-24"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U31/F12: Norway VKM (report 2017:9, published 29.03.2017): first case outside North America and South Korea (Nordfjella reindeer, April 2016); risk from eating infected meat \"seems to be very low\"; can't say whether the moose-type is safer or less safe; recommends culling a confineable host population.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b2-25"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U31: the cull was about 2,000 reindeer, and the sub-population was eradicated in 2018. Not on the VKM page; press and Veterinary Institute sources not fetched.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-b2-26"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U32/F12: Sweden (Livsmedelsverket report L 2020 nr 18): mainly an animal-health issue; no signs of spread to humans after 50 years; zoonotic potential uncertain, especially for untested Nordic variants; 33 Nordic cervids including four old Swedish moose; food law doesn't cover the hunter's own household; advice is not to eat suspected or confirmed animals, at home or in meat sold or donated.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b2-27"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F13/U49: the \"two hunters\" source is an AAN 2024 poster abstract with \"Methods: Not applicable\". It describes one 72-year-old (2022) with a postmortem diagnosis of sporadic CJD MM1; no prion characterisation was done; the friend appears only as \"recently died\". The authors say causation is unproven. UT Health later called it a case-report poster with \"no proven cases\" of transmission.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b2-28"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F13/U42: \"CDC reviewed both cases in 2022\". The source says CDC \"reviewed the 2022 cases\"; the deaths were in 2022 and the date of the review is not given. The Maddox lines are a statement to USA TODAY, republished on Yahoo. Correct line: \"CDC told USA TODAY it had reviewed the cases and found them consistent with classic sporadic CJD\". Error source: finder/engine rewording.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-b2-29"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U49: Maddox's other lines: hunting or eating venison doesn't mean someone got CJD that way; Colorado hunter studies show no increase.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b2-30"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U42: \"press accounts say the venison was never tested\". The press says the abstract does not say WHERE they hunted or WHETHER the deer was tested; it is not established that it was never tested. Correct line: \"there is no evidence the meat came from an infected deer\". Error source: finder rewording.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-b2-31"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U51/F14: the CDC hunter-cohort numbers (Abrams, 4th International CWD Symposium, Denver, 2023, preliminary). Colorado: 1,587,078 hunters, 19 prion deaths vs 19.4 expected; the CWD-endemic-licence subgroup (38%) 7 vs 6.3. Wyoming: 533,987 hunters, 7 vs 5.4. The ONLY source is the National Deer Association (a symposium sponsor) write-up dated 2023-07-10.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b2-32"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F14/U51: \"Wisconsin, 642 hunters KNOWN to have eaten positive venison, 0 prion deaths\". The 642 told surveyors they ate the venison OR INTENDED TO. Also, zero is the expected count: at about 1 case per million person-years, 5,778 person-years predicts about 0.006 cases (my arithmetic, from the NDA's own rate). The Wisconsin zero is reassuring only as \"nothing unusual yet\", not as evidence of safety. Error source: engine/finder wording.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-b2-33"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U58/F14: Oneida County, NY, March 2005 sportsmen's feast: 81 exposed people joined the baseline; six-year follow-up found no significant change in health conditions; it is still the only known large point-source exposure; CIDRAP (via Garruto, personal communication) says no prion disease to date. Nuance: the NDA says 29 participants who ATE the venison are followed (15-year report, 2020). Say \"about 80 people were exposed\", not \"81 ate it\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b2-34"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F15/U95: Minnesota DNR: prions resist heat and freezing; cooking or freezing won't remove them.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b2-35"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F15/U96: Wisconsin DNR: cooking doesn't deactivate prions; prions occur in muscle of late-stage deer; DHS advises not eating or giving away venison from CWD-affected areas UNTIL a test shows not detected. (The \"until\" clause is part of the advice; don't drop it.)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b2-36"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F15/U22/U91: the cooked-elk study (EID 31(2), Feb 2025). One CWD-positive 5-year-old bull elk, hunter-harvested. Grilling and boiling (medium-well) left seeding detectable and grilling increased detection; boiling water was positive; in a single-round human-PrP PMCA no meat converted human PrP, raw or cooked, which the authors read as \"limited zoonotic potential\". Added detail: in the same assay, BSE and one CWD isolate (a control) DID convert human PrP.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b2-37"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F15/U91 call it \"a 2025 CDC EID study\". It is a University of Texas Health Science Center at Houston study (Benavente…Morales) published in Emerging Infectious Diseases, CDC's journal; the journal disclaims that it is CDC's position. Say \"a study in a CDC journal\", or \"Texas researchers\". Error source: engine finding/finder.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-b2-38"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F15/U91 \"companion CDC EID paper… 10% bleach or Virkon S worked and dish soap was unreliable\". It is Milstein et al., EID 31(4) April 2025, University of Minnesota, not CDC. Bleach (10% and 40%) removed seeding everywhere. Virkon-S worked on knives and boards but only REDUCED it on grinders. Dish soap cleaned knives and cast-iron grinders but not cutting boards or stainless grinders. The authors note that seeding is not the same as infectivity. Error source: engine finding/finder.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-b2-39"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U68/F15: CDC's CJD infection-control steps: 1N NaOH plus a 121 °C autoclave for 30 min (or longer variants), 2N NaOH or undiluted bleach for heat-sensitive surfaces. CDC adds that real reprocessing methods haven't been rigorously tested. (The old URL redirects to /creutzfeldt-jakob/hcp/infection-control/.)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b2-40"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U82 (hunting-media counter-framing): Outdoor Life (2024-04-18) says there's no evidence CWD has infected hunters, which is accurate as \"no known cases\". The U82 quote \"unnecessarily caused alarm and fear\" is NOT in the article. The NDA line there is \"sketchy report… alarm and confusion\", about the 2024 abstract, not \"an earlier CWD report\". The NDA itself writes that the risk is \"low, but it's not zero\", so the hunting-media side does not claim zero risk. Error source: finder (misquote).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-b2-41"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U85 (advocacy framing): IG Wild beim Wild (anti-hunting group, 2026-06-01) headline \"new study shows transmission risk to humans\". U85's two admissions are confirmed. Its account of the study is wrong in three places: \"test animals were seven\" (18 were inoculated; seven analysed); \"elk and red deer\" (paper: elk, mule deer, white-tailed deer); prions found \"in all the test animals\" (paper: 6 of 7 brains). It omits the authors' \"do not imply efficient transmission\".","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-b2-42"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F18: Hunt 2009 (Wyoming), all 30 rifle-shot whitetail carcasses had metal fragments, ~136 per carcass (range 15–409). Trap holds: the abstract calls 136 a geometric mean and the results call it a median.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c1"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F18: fragments in the ground meat of 24 of 30 deer (80%); 74 of 234 ground packages (32%). 80% is DEER, 32% is PACKAGES. Per-deer share of packages ranged 0–100%.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c2"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F18: 93% of analysed fragments were lead (25 of 27, from 13 deer).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c3"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F18: clusters averaged 24 cm apart, max 45 cm. These are 2-D distances BETWEEN fragment clusters on a radiograph, not distance from the wound.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c4"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"Hunt 2009 setup, a script trap and the industry critique's target: one cartridge (7 mm Rem Mag, 150 gr, a single brand), Sheridan County WY, Nov 2007, each deer taken to a different commercial processor. The authors are mostly from The Peregrine Fund (a raptor/condor group).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c5"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F19: the Hunt 2009 blood-lead rise (2.29 vs 0.63) was in four PIGS fed fragment-containing venison, not people. (pypdf drops the µ, so the text reads \"mg/dL\"; the unit is µg/dL.)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c6"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"NEW (whole cuts are not zero-risk): in 4 of the 16 deer with fragments near the spine, fragments turned up in loin steaks. Most steak packages were never scanned. Only 3.1% of carcass fragments ended up in ground meat.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c7"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F18/U6: Minnesota DNR study design: 72 previously euthanized domestic SHEEP standing in for deer, shot broadside at 50 m, .308 with 150-grain bullets, July 2008.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c8"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F18 says \"exposed lead core 82, slug 28, copper or bonded 9\". WRONG: BONDED bullets are the 82 (DNR: \"bonded lead-core bullets (controlled expansion, exposed lead core)\"). The 9 is copper AND non-exposed-lead-core bullets, and those 9 are copper fragments. Ballistic tip 141, soft point 86, slug 28. Error source: engine finding wording (U6 itself is right).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-c9"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F18/U6: \"average maximum spread ~11 in (lead) vs 7 in (copper)\". 11 in holds only for the rapid-expansion lead bullets (ballistic tip, soft point). Bonded lead bullets averaged 9 in, slugs 5 in, muzzleloader 1 and 6 in. One ballistic-tip fragment reached 14 in. The copper bullets' few fragments sat under an inch from the exit wound, even though the copper/non-exposed group's average maximum was 7 in.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-c10"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"The \"up to 18 inches\" figure: low lead levels were found as far as 18 in from the exit hole. 18 in was the FARTHEST SAMPLING POINT (samples taken at 2, 10 and 18 in), so it is the edge of the measurement, not a measured outer limit. Sheep, not deer.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c11"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U7: only ~30% of fragments lay within 2 in of the exit wound. DNR can't recommend a trim distance, and rinsing moves lead around.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c12"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U5: DNR's hunter tips give no safe trim distance. Minnesota Agriculture found lead in 26% of 1,029 commercially ground packages vs 2% of 209 whole-muscle packages. The same sheet says lead in venison has not been linked to any illnesses (useful balance).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c13"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U60: Minnesota's 2008 screen of donated venison: 27% of ground vs 2% of whole-muscle had detectable metal, and a lab sub-sample showed it was lead. (DNR report says 27%; MDA's larger count on the tips sheet says 26%. Same story, two counts.)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c14"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U61: Wisconsin 2008: lead in 30 of 199 commercially processed/pantry samples (15%) and 8 of 98 hunter samples (8%); 7 of the 8 were ground; mean in positives 15.9 mg/kg. Two nuances. The \"FDA-recommended 0.05 mg/kg\" wording comes from the vet appeal letter; the consultation's own text calls 0.05 \"an international consensus standard … under discussion\". And Wisconsin's own June 2008 letter to pantries put the lead rate at \"about 4%\" (the 15% is normalised to 1-lb packages).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c15"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U0 (part 1): a Bismarck physician X-rayed 95 donated packages and found metal in 53; the Health Department's 5 targeted samples were all lead. NUANCE: the physician's own published paper (Cornatzer et al. 2009) reports 100 randomly selected packages, CT-scanned, 59 with fragments, drawn from 15,250 donated one-pound packages. Only 1 of 15 blind random biopsies held lead (120 ppm); the 5 image-guided biopsies aimed at visible metal ran 4,200–55,000 ppm.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c16"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U0 (part 2): ND's official follow-up: 404 ground-venison samples from 54 plants, 49 (12%) suspicious on X-ray, 51 sent to the lab (49 + 2 blanks), 24 with lead (5.94%), the other 27 bone, plastic or other metal. The figures are right but the SOURCE is wrong: they are not in the cited FAQ fact sheet but in ND's \"2008/09 Lead Fragments in Ground Venison Processors Study\" (Sept 2009). TRAP: the samples were ~¼-cup vials, not packages, so 5.94% can't be compared with package rates (the study itself says the 2008 and 2009 numbers can't be compared). Error source: finder citation.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-c17"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U1/F19: Iqbal 2009 (CDC/ND): geometric mean blood lead 1.27 vs 0.84 µg/dL (eaters vs non-eaters); adjusted gap 0.30 µg/dL (95% CI 0.16–0.44); ages 2–92. 0.30 is ~9% of 3.5 (arithmetic, 8.6%). Read from the ABSTRACT only. The state fact sheet says 738 participants; the paper says 742 recruited and 736 tested.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c18"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U62: the study design figures are right (742/736, 80.8% ate game, 98.8% of it hunted). WRONG: \"servings of 2 oz or more were linked to higher blood lead\" overstates it. The serving-size link was significant for \"other game\" ONLY, not venison. Recency (<1 month) held for all game types. Error source: finder compression of the abstract.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-c19"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U63: blood lead in the ND sample ran from none detectable to 9.82 µg/dL. The state stresses that the link is statistical and adjusted, and that wild game is not the only or most important risk factor. The fact sheet does NOT say \"a few individuals exceeded 3.5\": at least one did (9.82), but the count is unknown. The finder inferred it.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-c20"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U2: CDC's child blood lead reference value is 3.5 µg/dL (from 5.0 on Oct 28, 2021), the 97.5th percentile of US children aged 1–5 (NHANES 2015–2018). It is not a health-based standard, and no safe level has been identified.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c21"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U3/U101/F19: ND Health: pregnant women and children under 6 should not eat any venison taken with lead bullets; others should minimise exposure and use judgment; non-lead bullets are the surest fix. The sheet is footed \"October 2008\" (dossier says Nov). ND repeated the advice in Sept 2009.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c22"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U101: ND's pantry rule (lead-shot venison as whole cuts only; bow-shot whole or ground). ND relays the Minnesota study's finding that most particles are too small to see, feel or sense when chewing. ND also said that had these fragments been in beef, the meat would have been recalled.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c23"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U4: Minnesota Dept of Health (Oct 2008): pregnant women and children under 6 should not eat food-shelf venison or any venison taken with lead ammunition; under-6s absorb lead more easily; lead crosses the placenta; most fragments were in ground venison.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c24"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U92: Wisconsin's current DNR page does say under-6s and pregnant women face the highest risk, to trim (no distance), that ground venison may carry higher risk, and to minimise rinsing. WRONG: non-lead is \"the best way\" to eliminate risk, not \"the only way\". Also, the June 2008 DHS letter was NOT softer than ND/MN: it told both groups to \"avoid\" lead-shot venison. The softer wording (\"use caution\") is the current DNR page. Error source: finder paraphrase. (The DHS Lead-Safe sources page was not checked.)","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-c25"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"NEW: Michigan MDHHS family handout: women of childbearing age and children 6 and under should avoid venison taken with high-velocity lead bullets; older children and adults should take care with ground venison; it also says avoid acidic marinades. The handout is undated.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c26"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"NEW, and it weakens the dossier's \"guidance is 15+ years old\" caveat: New York DOH donated-venison notice (code 10/25, i.e. Oct 2025): pregnant people and children should choose other meats; up to 20% of donated venison may contain lead; ground venison is fine for adults not planning pregnancy.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c27"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U102: CDC's lead-and-breastfeeding page says to limit game meat such as deer hunted with lead ammunition. The other half of U102 (that CDC's main lead pages never mention ammunition) is an absence claim I did NOT check.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c28"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U102/F19: ATSDR's 2008 Wisconsin consultation modelled a significant risk of elevated blood lead in children eating lead-shot venison but called the hazard \"indeterminate\". Wisconsin DHS called non-lead ammunition the simplest and most effective fix.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c29"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"Later human studies (abstracts only; F19 says none were checked). Norway 2013: monthly-or-more cervid meat was linked to ~31% higher blood lead, mostly from minced meat. Italy 2017 (95 people): no link to game meat after adjustment (hunting itself was). S. Germany 2022: game eaters slightly higher (medians 21.3 vs 20.0 µg/L). NZ 2018: one subsistence hunter at 74.7 µg/dL, which fell after switching to non-lead. Argentina 2026: children's blood lead linked to game meat (mostly duck and capybara, not deer). None measures a health outcome from venison.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c30"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"Industry critique (NSSF 2008 statement on the CDC study, as reproduced) checked against the paper. \"Statistically insignificant 0.3 difference\" is WRONG: the 95% CI of 0.16–0.44 excludes zero. \"CDC's level of concern … is 10\" was true in 2008 but has been superseded (reference value 3.5 since 2021, no safe level). \"ND did not conduct their own study\" is PARTLY true: ND tested only 5 targeted samples from the physician's packages in March 2008, then ran its own 404-sample study. The NSSF's \"children under 6 mean 0.88\" and \"non-random control group\" could not be checked (abstract only). Error source: published critique.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-c31"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U64/F20: Stokke et al. (Ambio 2019): 729 lead-bullet and 84 copper-bullet records; species were roe deer, red deer, brown bear and moose; flight distances similar. NUANCE: the 2.8 g vs ~0.5 g mass-loss figure is for bullets retrieved from MOOSE and is cited from an earlier paper (Stokke 2017), not measured in these 813 records. The paper confirms that copper costs more and that hunters worry about supply and cost.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-c32"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"Counter-evidence to F20's \"no measured loss of killing power\". Hampton et al. 2023 (276 sambar deer, Australia): single-shot kills and wounding were similar (lead-free 0.89 vs lead 0.85; wounding <4% both). But among single thoracic shots, deer hit with lead-free bullets ran 56% farther (35 m vs 22 m). The authors still concluded welfare outcomes were similar. F20 should say \"similar welfare outcomes\", not \"no measured difference\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c33"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"German Federal Institute for Risk Assessment (BfR) poster (Martin et al.): escape distance not significantly affected by lead vs non-lead in roe deer or wild boar; shot placement and distance mattered more. A poster, not a full paper.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c34"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U8/F20: California requires non-lead for all wildlife taken with a firearm since July 1, 2019; non-lead is sold at most gun and sporting-goods stores, and stores can special-order. CDFW says non-lead is the same price as premium lead (sometimes less). The \"< $10 per box\" over cheap lead is only \"for some calibers and gauges\", and CDFW concedes non-lead \"can cost more\" than some lead. The dossier generalises the $10 figure. This is the agency's own framing.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-c35"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U65/F20: Arizona condor range, 2011: 77% non-lead use in the voucher units (12 and 13) vs 49% in non-voucher units 9 and 10 (mail survey, 980 of 1,500 returned), which the authors call a ceiling. NOT IN THE CITED PAPER: the \"mandatory checkpoints\" method and the USGS survey of natural-resource professionals (40.5%/68.5%). Error source: finder (likely another source merged in).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-c36"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U83: WTIP (Apr 2026), Minnesota: all donated venison is X-rayed; deer from mandatory-CWD areas must test negative; 2025 brought 13,883 lb from 403 deer, up from 7,371 lb in 2022. NOT IN THE ARTICLE: \"the x-ray rule began in 2008 after random testing found lead in more than 5% of donated whole-cut venison, double the prior year\". That figure is absent, so the dossier's \"WTIP vs DNR 2%\" conflict is moot. Error source: finder.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-c37"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"Pounds DISCARDED after screening: Minnesota Agriculture's meat-inspection director said X-ray screening throws away about 10% of donated venison on average (over the six years to 2022). Iowa requires a lead warning label on donated venison; Kansas, Missouri and Nebraska did not (2022). This is a secondary news quote of an official.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c38"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"Total pounds pulled or destroyed in the 2008 ND and MN pantry recalls: no figure in any saved primary. Confirmed only that ND told pantries not to distribute ground venison, that MN ordered food shelves to destroy it, and that WI told pantries to test or discard. Unsaved search snippets mention ~17,000 lb donated in ND that year and ~4,000 lb on hand; those are NOT verified. Scale anchor that IS verified: Cornatzer's sample came from 15,250 donated one-pound packages (C16).","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-c39"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U29: Maryland Ch. 309 (HB 175, 2026): a nonrefundable credit for up to $75 of processing per donated antlerless deer; yearly cap raised from $300 to $600 (no cap for deer-management-permit deer); no carry-over; donor liability immunity; effective July 1, 2026; revenue loss ~$125,000/yr in FY2027–29; nothing on CWD or lead. The $600 is CONFIRMED by the legislative history. The bill as introduced repealed the cap; the House amended it to keep the cap at $600; the Senate tried to restore the repeal; the House refused and the Senate receded. The peg hunt's X1 line (\"repeals the limit on raising the yearly cap\") misread struck text in the chapter PDF. The governor approved it April 28, 2026.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c40"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"U104 (lead half only): Wisconsin says fragments are too small to see or feel, mostly near the wound but some spread farther, grinding spreads them, minimise rinsing, trim around the wound, and under-6s and pregnant women face the highest risk. The CWD-testing half belongs to B1 and was not checked.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c41"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"F19's \"how much this lead matters is unresolved\" stands on the primaries: ND says nobody knows how much fragment lead the body absorbs; Minnesota DNR says lead in venison has not been linked to any illness; ATSDR calls the hazard \"indeterminate\". F19's framing (small on average, real caution for under-6s and pregnancy) is fair. The dossier's 2008–2010 date on all guidance is out of date (see C27).","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-c42"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(U9, F21) A national survey of 914 US whitetails found Toxoplasma antibodies in 36%, and live parasite came from the hearts of deer in 11 states. The authors say undercooked venison should not be eaten. Sampling ran 2012-2019 and covered 19 states.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(U11, F21) The 2017 Wisconsin retreat: the venison was served deliberately undercooked. 9 of 11 who ate it fell ill (12 attendees in all), and a third had eye symptoms. The strain was an atypical wildlife genotype (Clin Infect Dis 2021, CDC Stacks record).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(U10, F21) The 2018 Quebec hunters at an Illinois retreat: 6 of 10 got acute toxoplasmosis, and 5 of those 6 ate rare steak (1 ate it medium). 2 hunters who ate well-done meat stayed well. The leftover frozen meat tested negative.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d3"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(U66) CDC's toxoplasmosis page names undercooked meat as a route. It says to cook to a safe internal temperature and that freezing at 0 F for several days greatly reduces the chance of infection. It does not mention venison or give a temperature. The Quebec paper adds that freezing destroys the cysts.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d4"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(U45, F21) Oregon 1995 venison jerky: 6 confirmed and 5 presumptive O157 cases. The strain matched the jerky, raw meat from the same deer, the saw and the hide. Live bacteria survived drying for up to 10 h at 62.8 C (about 145 F). Read from the PubMed abstract (Keene et al., JAMA 1997), not the dossier's secondary catalogue page.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d5"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(U12, F21) The finder said CDC's Connecticut child paper was \"only the second published O157-from-venison report\". WRONG: the paper calls itself the first report linking illness to O157 in WHITE-TAILED deer. It lists two earlier venison links, the 1995 jerky (black-tailed deer) and a 1987 Washington case, so it is at least the third. The error came from the finder's paraphrase. The case details are right: a boy of 7, undercooked tenderloin, and O157 found in frozen meat from the same carcass 4 weeks later.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d6"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(U12, F21) A Georgia field study found O157 in 4% of hunter-killed whitetails. Deer carry it more often in fall and winter, which is hunting season.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d7"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(U80, F21) Oregon 2017-18: 5 O157 cases were tied to deer from one Douglas County hunting unit, the first such link. Note: it was found by genome-matching in 2019 and published in Public Health Reports in 2022 (DOI 2021). The dossier relied on a sciencegate mirror; this is the PubMed abstract.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d8"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(U13, F21) Trichinella, 2008-2012: 84 US cases, of which deer accounted for 2 and bear 41. CDC calls herbivore links possibly \"a best guess\". Freezing does not reliably kill T. nativa.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d9"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(U14, F22) Penn State Extension: cool the carcass fast to 35-40 F, wear gloves, hold it at 40 F or below for 2-3 days at most. Cook whole cuts to 145 F, ground to 160 F, stews and leftovers to 165 F, and pre-cook jerky meat to 160 F.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d10"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(U44, F22) The federal foodsafety.gov chart lists venison, wild or farm-raised, at 160 F. The dossier had this only via secondary quotes; it is now read at the primary. That settles F22's \"disputed\" temperature: the federal figure is 160 F, and Penn State's 145 F is the outlier.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d11"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(new, F22) CDC's own trichinellosis report says the 145 F whole-cut rule EXCLUDES wild game. That is a second federal source against airing 145 F for venison.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d12"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(U15, F23) Michigan's 2018 Clark's Marsh \"Do Not Eat\" advisory came from ONE deer of 20, with 547 ppb PFOS in MUSCLE against a 300 ppb action level. The other 19 were low or none. The radius was about 5 miles.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d13"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(U16, F23) Michigan's 2021 follow-up: in 44 deer from 2020, PFOS peaked at 82.6 ppb in muscle and 2,970 in liver. The 22 hunter muscle samples from 2019 had none. The area shrank to 3 miles. The statewide advice is still not to eat deer liver or kidneys.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d14"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(U17, F23) Wisconsin, Marinette (JCI/Tyco), 2020, 20 deer: PFOS showed in the muscle of 1 deer (2.67 ng/g) but in the liver of all 20 (3.83-92.0). The result was Do Not Eat for liver within 5 miles; muscle and heart were not a concern.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d15"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(U25, U67, F23) Wisconsin, Town of Stella (Oneida County), September 2025: at most one meal a month of venison within 5 miles, and Do Not Eat for liver. It rests on 11 hunter-donated deer from 2024.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d16"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(U67, F23) \"Stella is the first Wisconsin advisory on the meat itself.\" Probably true: the only other one, Marinette, covers liver only. But neither the advisories page nor the release says \"first\". This is the finder's inference, so don't air \"first\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d17"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(new, F23) Wisconsin checked for a statewide problem: in 32 livers from 32 counties, only one had any PFAS, and there is no statewide liver advisory. This supports \"local, not wild deer everywhere\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d18"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(U18, F23) Maine is the strictest: Do Not Eat for ALL deer and wild turkey meat and organs in four areas. Cooking or trimming doesn't help. History: 8 deer in October 2021, a ~125 sq mi area cut to 25 sq mi in 2023 with turkey added, and three more areas added in 2024-2025.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d19"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(pass orders) \"Maine's statewide advisory\". WRONG: Maine's PFAS Do Not Eat is local, covering parts of 8 towns in four areas. The STATEWIDE advice in this domain is Michigan's not-the-liver-or-kidneys line (D14). The dossier itself has it right (\"now four areas\"); the error is in the orders' wording.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d20"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(U18) The Maine numbers: liver about 624-809 ng/g, and \"1-2 meals a year for a child, 4-5 for an adult\". These are not on the advisory page (they came from a layout-extracted table). The page only says the worst deer warranted fewer than 2-3 meals a year. Keep them off air.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-d21"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(F23 trap) Does the Marinette PFAS area overlap the county of Wisconsin's first CWD deer? Same county: both are in Marinette County near Peshtigo/Marinette. Nothing places the CWD buck inside the PFAS 5-mile circle. Say \"the same county\", never \"the same deer area\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d22"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(U94, F24) North Dakota Game and Fish: EHD/HD does not infect people. There is no risk from handling or eating meat from infected animals, but secondary infection can spoil the meat. Don't shoot or eat a sick-looking animal. The New York \"cooking kills it\" sub-claim was not checked.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d23"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(U94, F24) Michigan DNR, 2026-09-09 (first 2026 EHD cases: Branch, Ingham, Jackson, Lenawee): venison from EHD areas is safe, but don't eat visibly sick animals.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d24"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(U19, F24) APHIS, 2026-10-21: the first NWS detection in US wildlife was an elk on a Pecos County, TX game ranch, reported after a hunter harvested it. The page is about spotting and reporting and says nothing on eating.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d25"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(U20, F24) The APHIS hunter fact sheet (APHIS-25-037, June 2026) lists compromised meat quality and possible transport limits on meat and carcasses, and says not to handle a suspect animal. It has no statement that the meat is dangerous to eat.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d26"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(peg-hunt gap) USDA's OWN \"not a food safety issue\" wording, found at the primary (the peg hunt had it only from news). Screwworm.gov, which redirects to APHIS, says it is not a food safety issue, and USDA's June 2026 releases say screwworms do not infest meat. TRAP: USDA ties this to FSIS inspection of the COMMERCIAL supply, which a hunter's deer never goes through. It is not a ruling on eating an infested wild deer.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d27"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(U103, F24) CDC: the screwworm route is a fly laying eggs in open wounds and body openings, not eating.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d28"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(U93, F24) Texas Parks and Wildlife does not recommend eating sick or infested wildlife. It encourages deboning meat from animals taken in infested areas and bars moving infested parts out. This is precaution, not a statement that the meat is poisonous.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d29"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(U50, F25) The worked example. The source deer is a MAINE buck (Bangor Daily News, 2025): 103 lb dressed, \"just over 51 lb\" boneless, a $155 processor fee, about $3/lb. The writer concludes hunting saves money because he ignores license and gear. The dossier's $3.6-4.1/lb adds WI/MN/MI license fees to a 50 lb yield. That is the finder's arithmetic; with 51 lb it is $3.51 (WI), $3.73 (MN), $4.04 (MI combo). The direction is right but the figure is one newspaper deer.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d30"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(U69) License fees: Wisconsin resident gun deer license $24 (Fall 2026-Spring 2027 regulations); Minnesota adult firearms deer license $34 by statute. The \"+$1 agent fee\" for Minnesota is NOT in the statute text read and was not verified.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d31"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(U69) Michigan base license $11 plus deer combo $40 (two tags). These match the eRegulations digest, but that page carries no year. A web search found 2026 fee-increase proposals ($11→$17 base, $20→$30 deer) reported in news, status unclear. Treat as the last confirmed schedule, not as 2026 fact.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d32"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(U24, F25) BLS ground beef (100% beef), September 2026, $6.792/lb. The FIGURE is right, but the dossier's cited series APU0000703613 is the SIRLOIN series; ground beef is APU0000703112. The citation error came from the finder.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d33"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(U24, F25) BLS USDA Choice boneless sirloin, September 2026, $14.106/lb (FRED, updated 2026-10-14).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d34"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(U50, F25) \"About $160 of gear, fuel or lease erases the ground-beef saving.\" This is arithmetic: 51 lb × $6.792 = $346, minus $179 (WI fee plus license) = about $167, or about $156 for MN. Roughly right, but it is derived from D30, D31 and D33.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d35"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(new; this fills the dossier's F25 gap \"no FWS per-hunter spending figure\") FWS 2022: big-game hunters averaged $1,352 each on trips and equipment, excluding land leases. By arithmetic, $1,352 over one Ohio-average deer (52.19 lb) is about $26/lb, nearly double Choice sirloin ($14.11). Caveats: big game includes elk, bear and turkey; it is per hunter, not per deer; some hunters get several deer and many get none.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d36"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(U26, F26) The National Deer Association counts 6,194,659 US deer harvested in 2024-25: 3,047,741 antlered and 3,146,918 antlerless. That is the fifth highest on record and the highest since 2020. NDA is a hunting-advocacy group compiling state data; attribute it to them.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d37"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(U27, F26) Ohio Division of Wildlife weighed 115 deer from the 2017 gun season: 107.56 lb dressed on average, giving 52.19 lb of venison (48.52%). Bucks gave 62.51 lb, antlerless deer 45.05 lb.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d38"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(U27, F26) The derived 323 million lb, 0.95 lb per American and 1.6% of beef. Re-computed: 6,194,659 × 52.19 = 323.3M lb, about 0.95 lb at 340M people, and 1.61% of 59.1 lb. The arithmetic holds, but it multiplies a national count by one Ohio sample, and the population is an assumption.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d39"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(U70, F26) USDA ERS (July 2025 outlook): beef disappearance was 59.1 retail lb per person in 2024, with 2025 forecast at 59.1 and 2026 at 58.4. Side note: the dossier's \"27,789 million lb beef production\" is the PORK row in this table, but that figure isn't needed for the script.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d40"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(U28, F26) FWS 2022: 11.5 million big-game hunters, 80% of 14.4 million hunters, about 1 in 23 Americans aged 16+.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d41"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(U28, F26) 75% of Michigan residents have eaten wild-harvested meat at least once, including 59% of nonhunters, and 96% of those had eaten venison (Goguen and Riley, Wildlife Society Bulletin; 2014 survey of 983). The source read is the journal publisher's news write-up, not the paper itself. TRAP: its photo caption wrongly says \"in the past year\"; the measure is lifetime.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d42"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(U28) Texas: 98% of successful hunters share the meat, reaching about 3.7 million people outside their households. Not fetched; keep off air.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-d43"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(U52, U86, F26) NSSF (December 2025): there is no national database. Six state programs gave \"roughly 1.06 million lb in a single year\", and the national total is \"likely\" over 2 million lb (about 0.62% of 323M lb by arithmetic). Caveat: NSSF calls the 1.06M a 2024-25 total, but its own components mix in 2023-24 seasons (MO, VA, WV). NSSF is the firearms-industry trade group; attribute it as an estimate.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d44"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(U71, F26) Wisconsin's deer donation program: over 100,000 deer and over 4 million lb of ground venison since 2000, and donating is free.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d45"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(U29, F26) Maryland Chapter 309 (HB 175, 2026): a nonrefundable income-tax credit for up to $75 of butchering costs per donated antlerless deer, through a 501(c)(3) program. It takes effect 2026-07-01, applies from tax year 2026, was approved 2026-04-28, and sunsets with the 2024 law (June 30, 2029; through tax year 2028).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d46"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(U29, F26; the peg hunt's lost strikethrough) The yearly CAP: the enacted law RAISES it from $300 to $600, and the cap does not apply to deer taken on a deer management permit. Bill history: as introduced, HB 175 bracketed the whole cap paragraph, i.e. repealed the cap outright. The committee amendment struck those brackets, kept the cap, and replaced $300 with $600. The chapter PDF's extraction prints \"[(1) … $300 $600 … (2)]\" because the struck brackets and struck $300 lose their strike-through. The DLS fiscal note (Third Reader, Revised) states the result in plain words. So the dossier's \"$600 a year\" is right, and an \"unlimited\" reading of the chapter text would be wrong.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d47"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(U29) Chapter 309 says nothing about CWD or lead testing; the full two pages were read. DLS estimates the credit costs $125,000 a year in FY2027-2029. Arithmetic: $600 ÷ $75 = 8 donated deer to reach the cap.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d48"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"(F21 framing, backs the dossier's own trap) The outbreak authors themselves call venison clusters rare, and no source here gives a yearly count of venison illnesses. Air them as \"it happens, here's how\", never as a rate or a ranking against beef.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d49"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"Minnesota: the hunter test is run on two lymph nodes from the head and neck, sent to a certified lab. The only two results are \"detected\" or \"not detected\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-1"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"Wisconsin's state lab (WVDL) tests the medial retropharyngeal lymph nodes. If any other tissue arrives, no test is run. Samples must be frozen and shipped only Monday to Thursday so they don't decompose in transit. In other words, sample quality is the hunter's job.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-2"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"The US standard: USDA has approved ELISA, IHC and Western blot. Nearly every lab screens hunters' samples with ELISA and confirms positives with IHC. (Source: CIDRAP's 2025 report, which Minnesota DNR paid for under contract. It is a review, not an agency rule.)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-3"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"Minnesota runs the second (confirming) test only when the screening test is positive. So a \"not detected\" rests on the screening test alone, about three days at the lab. Times are B1-25's. The \"screen only\" reading is mine, from the DNR's own sequence.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-4"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"ELISA-positive wild deer from areas where CWD is established are usually not sent for confirmation. Confirmation is advised when CWD turns up in a new area, herd unit or species, as with Michigan's new counties (T1-6).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-5"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"Michigan: the MSU Veterinary Diagnostic Laboratory runs and confirms the state's tests. A first-in-county case also goes to USDA's National Veterinary Services Laboratory in Ames, Iowa. Free self-sample kits go to the MSU lab, and fee testing goes to USDA-approved labs (cost is B1-27's).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-6"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"Wisconsin: the Wisconsin Veterinary Diagnostic Laboratory (UW) runs the DNR's samples (a news report citing the DNR's 2025 summary). The same lab offers paid tests, reports results to the hunter and the DNR, and says results may take up to two weeks.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-7"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"Minnesota: which lab runs the DNR's free tests is not named on any page read. The DNR says \"a certified laboratory\" and lists the University of Minnesota, Colorado State and the University of Wisconsin for paid tests. In a study of 2019-20 samples, Minnesota's wild-deer lymph nodes were screened at Colorado State's lab. That is study context, not proof of the current arrangement. Don't name a Minnesota lab on air.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-t1-8"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"RT-QuIC, the newer amplification test, is not USDA-approved for diagnosing CWD in deer, and states are reluctant to switch before approval. Commercial RT-QuIC kits do exist. Don't present RT-QuIC as what hunters get from the state.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-9"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"Michigan's own deer, 1,300 lymph nodes (MSU with the Michigan DNR, 2022): ELISA flagged 184, RT-QuIC 178, IHC confirmed 176. The 7 disagreements (0.5%) were all ELISA-positive and IHC-negative, so false alarms, not missed cases. The three tests agree closely on the deer they CAN see.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-10"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"The ELISA validation study (Colorado, 4,175 paired samples): measured against IHC, ELISA caught 98.3-100% of positives in lymph nodes and 92.1-93.3% in brainstem (obex), and the misses came only in early-stage cases. Caveat: this sensitivity is relative to IHC, which has its own early-stage blind spot (T1-18).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-11"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"Without assuming any test is perfect (a Bayesian model, Minnesota deer, 2022), the standard ELISA/IHC on lymph nodes was estimated at about 91-92% sensitivity in wild deer and RT-QuIC at about 92-95%. TRAP: this rests on only 61 wild and 46 farmed deer and comes with wide uncertainty. Never air \"the test misses one infected deer in twelve\" as a measured fact.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-12"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"The same paper's plain statement: the tests are good at late-stage deer and weaker early on, when there are few prions in the tissue.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-13"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"CIDRAP's 2025 summary: early-stage sensitivity \"may be low\", and how accurate the field test really is remains unknown.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-14"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"Why the lymph node is the tissue tested: in 4,430 Wisconsin deer tested both ways, 1 positive in 5 (20.4%) showed prions ONLY in the lymph node, and none showed them only in the brainstem. The node catches cases earlier than the brain does.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-15"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"How soon the node shows it: in fawns dosed by mouth (a lab study using a sensitivity-boosted IHC), prions appeared in throat and gut lymph tissue \"as early as 42 days\" after exposure and in every fawn examined from day 53 on. The obvious inference, which is mine: a deer infected in the weeks before the hunt can carry too little in the node to detect.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-16"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"In infected mule deer, prions build up \"relatively rapidly\" in lymph tissue and later in the nervous system. One common prion-gene type (225SF) builds up more slowly in the lymph tissue too, so how early a test can see it varies from deer to deer.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-17"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"A sampling blind spot: IHC looks at one slice of lymph node, which can miss an early infection. An earlier study found ELISA and IHC disagreed on 12 of 101 lymph nodes, and showed that testing the node on the other side mattered.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-18"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"The brief's \"Not in the record\" note and the dossier's gap say \"No sensitivity or early-infection data was found\". WRONG, that data exists: ELISA-vs-IHC validation (T1-11), a model estimate without a gold standard (T1-12), lymph node vs brainstem (T1-15), time-course studies (T1-16, T1-17) and CIDRAP's review (T1-14). What is truly missing is a measured miss rate for naturally infected deer in their first weeks. The brief's advice still holds: say \"testing tells you if your deer was positive\", not \"a negative test makes it safe\". The error came from the engine's gap note.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-t1-19"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"Results under the USDA-approved methods come back as positive, not detected or \"unsuitable\". There is no \"CWD-free\" result. (Penn State's Deer-Forest Study, a university blog, undated. Minnesota's own pages name only detected or not detected, T1-1.)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-20"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"How often hunters' samples are unsuitable (wrong tissue, rotten, unlabeled): no published rate was found for Wisconsin, Michigan or Minnesota. What IS sourced: the wrong tissue means no test (T1-2). Don't air a rate.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-t1-21"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"Wisconsin DNR, in its own words: a \"not detected\" result does not guarantee the deer isn't infected, but makes it less likely (Tami Ryan, then acting wildlife bureau director). APPROXIMATE because it is RMEF's 2019-09-20 reprint of the DNR release; the original could not be fetched. Fine to quote with \"Wisconsin's DNR has said\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t1-22"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"The clearest agency wording found, from Virginia's wildlife agency (outside the peg states): the tests are not food-safety tests, early infections may sit below the detection limit, and \"no prions detected\" makes infection \"considerably less likely\" without guaranteeing it.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-23"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"Minnesota: \"not a food safety test\" and \"the hunter's choice\" are B1-26's (cited). New here: the DNR's 2025 hunter leaflet repeats \"hunter's choice\" and says to keep the venison \"separate and identifiable\" until the result arrives. That is the practical step that makes a positive result manageable.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-24"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"\"Neither USDA test is a food safety test.\" This is Penn State's statement. No APHIS or NVSL page saying it in its own words was found. Attribute it to Penn State, or use the state wording (T1-23, B1-26), never to \"USDA\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t1-25"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"The strongest reassurance on record, from a public-health scientist rather than an agency: Michael Osterholm (CIDRAP, University of Minnesota), in written testimony to the Minnesota House around 2019, said \"not detected\" makes prions \"highly unlikely\". The same document predicts human cases. It is opinion on both counts, so pair the two if either is used.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-26"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"Why a missed infection is not a zero question: infectious prions have been found in the skeletal muscle of CWD deer (Science, 2006). Prion protein has also been found in the muscle of whitetails showing no recognised signs, at roughly 2,000-10,000 times lower than in brain, sitting in the nerve fibres in the muscle (PLoS One, 2011). Wisconsin's \"late-stage\" wording is B1-32. \"No clinical signs\" is not the same as early infection, so don't air \"early-infected deer have prions in their meat\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-27"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"Wisconsin, if a result comes back POSITIVE: the hunter is told by email (or by letter). DHS advises eating only not-detected venison. Throw unwanted carcass waste OR PACKAGED MEAT in a landfill that accepts it. A replacement harvest authorization arrives within 2-4 days. Clean equipment with soap and water, then soak in 50/50 bleach: an hour for non-steel items, 5 minutes for steel.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-28"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"Michigan, if POSITIVE: the hunter is told by letter or phone and gets a replacement antlerless licence. The DNR relays CDC advice that infected animals not be eaten \"by either humans or domestic animals\", so no scraps for the dog. Carcass parts go to a landfill or bagged trash.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-29"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"Minnesota, if POSITIVE: the DNR contacts the hunter (only not-detected results are posted online). Processors ask whether the hunter still wants the meat. If not, it is disposed of. If so, it is processed alone, never batched (B1-39, cited). Osterholm's testimony says the Health Department advises against eating it but leaves the decision to the hunter.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-30"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"If some meat was ALREADY eaten or given away before a positive result: no Wisconsin, Michigan, Minnesota, CDC or USDA page read here says what to do after eating it, or tells the hunter to notify people who were given meat. The only advice is BEFORE the fact: don't share until the result comes back (Wisconsin DHS, B1-32), keep it separate and identifiable (T1-24), have it processed on its own (CDC, B1-33). Wisconsin's \"packaged meat\" disposal line (T1-28) is the nearest thing to an after-the-fact step. The script must not invent a \"call your doctor\" or \"tell your neighbour\" rule.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-t1-31"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"Processor and donation batches: the Wisconsin donation program lets the processor hold donated deer or venison until results come in. Wisconsin processors MAY batch only not-detected deer (B1-37), Minnesota guidance says hold carcass or trim until results (B1-39), and the CDC says ask for individual processing (B1-33). One positive deer in a mixed batch of sausage would put a family's trim in question. That inference is mine; no agency spells it out.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-32"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"Why \"it looked healthy\" is no comfort: per Minnesota's leaflet, the deer looks healthy through most of the infection, signs may take years to show, and the incubation period is 1½ to 3 years. (Same point as B1-29 and B1-31, from the 2025 leaflet.)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-33"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"Minnesota turnaround, a refinement of B1-25 (which quotes the FAQ's 7-14 days): the results look-up page itself says 10-14 BUSINESS days, longer at busy times. Air \"about one to two weeks, sometimes longer\", never a single number.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t1-34"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"B1-28 says no Michigan page gives a turnaround, including the DeerCheck page. WRONG: the saved DeerCheck page (the 2026 sample-site list, drop boxes \"Oct. 1 through Jan. 4\", matching B1-15) says results \"may take 6-8 weeks\". For a Michigan family, the eat-before-the-result question can mean weeks, not days. The 2017 \"seven business days\" figure is out of date and should not be used. The error came from B1's read of its own saved file.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-t1-35"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"\"7,000-15,000 CWD-positive animals eaten every year\": a rough estimate from a 2017 white paper by the Alliance for Public Wildlife, relayed in Osterholm's testimony. The white paper was not opened and the figure is advocacy-sourced. Don't air it (it would also be T3's question).","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-t1-36"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"What the UK government actually told the public for ten years (the Inquiry's own summary): \"no evidence\", \"most unlikely\" and \"safe to eat beef\". The word it used was not \"impossible\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-1"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"The Southwood Working Party's report (submitted 9 Feb 1989, published 27 Feb 1989, the date of the Hansard answer) concluded the risk to humans \"appears remote\" and BSE was \"most unlikely\" to affect human health. Wording verified in both the Inquiry and the government's own 1989 statement to Parliament.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-2"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"The 1989 reassurance rested on an ANALOGY TO A DIFFERENT DISEASE: BSE probably came from scrapie and would behave like it, and scrapie had not infected humans in 200+ years. That assumption turned out to be wrong.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-3"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"Southwood did warn that being wrong would be very serious. The Inquiry found the warning \"was lost from sight\" and the report kept being cited as proof of remote risk. The Working Party admitted it had \"little evidence to go on\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-4"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"The same 1989 government statement already called the risk \"very remote\", said there was \"no evidence of any link\" to CJD, and said the CMO was setting up a watch for changes in CJD. That watch is what later caught vCJD (T2-31).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-5"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"May 1990: a Siamese cat died of a BSE-like disease and media alarm followed. On 16 May 1990 BBC Newsnight showed agriculture minister John Gummer trying to feed his four-year-old daughter a beefburger. The Inquiry did NOT criticise Gummer's 15 May press releases, because they said beef was safe only BECAUSE of the slaughter policy and the offal ban. Nor did it criticise the burger stunt.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-6"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"16 May 1990: Chief Medical Officer Sir Donald Acheson said beef \"can be eaten safely by everyone\", and on TV that there was \"no risk\". The Inquiry found that his statement, unlike Gummer's, left out the precondition and was likely to convey \"BSE is no risk to human health\": false reassurance.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-7"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"December 1995, about three months before the announcement: Health Secretary Stephen Dorrell agreed on TV that there was \"no conceivable risk\" from what was then in the food chain. He later regretted it, and the Inquiry called it more extreme than he could justify. In October 1995 the CMO (Calman) had again said beef was safe to eat.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-8"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"The Inquiry's headline verdict: the government did NOT lie. It genuinely believed the risk was remote, but the \"campaign of reassurance was a mistake\", and the public felt betrayed when the link was announced.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-9"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"How the risk was communicated: as evidence against the scrapie theory grew, the public was never told that the perceived likelihood had changed. Statements about beef conveyed that BSE itself was \"not transmissible\". Dissenting scientists were treated with derision.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-10"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"Behind the reassurance, precautions WERE taken: the offal ban came in late 1989. By 1994 the expert committee judged the risk remote only BECAUSE of those precautions, but that was never put to the public. The Inquiry also found the reassurance made the people enforcing the precautions less careful. A lesson that transfers to CWD: reassurance can undercut the precaution it depends on.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-11"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"The viewer's framing, \"they said mad cow couldn't jump to people\", is mostly fair in substance. The formal line was \"remote\" / \"most unlikely\" / \"no evidence\", never a scientific claim that it was impossible. But senior officials did say \"no risk\" (Acheson, TV, 1990) and \"no conceivable risk\" (Dorrell, 1995), and the Inquiry found the overall message was that BSE was not transmissible. Then it did cross: \"probably\" in 1996, \"clearly established\" by 2000. Fair wording: \"they told us the risk was remote and beef was safe; it wasn't\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t2-12"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"Michael Osterholm (CIDRAP director), in written testimony to the Minnesota House (undated; its newest reference is 2019), makes the same argument as the commenter: British officials and industry said BSE cattle were safe to eat and claimed the \"species barrier\" would prevent transmission. The Inquiry records the reasoning as the scrapie analogy and \"remote\" risk (T2-3), not a species-barrier guarantee, so his framing is a fair gist but not the record's wording. His figure of \"more than 200\" vCJD patients is right (T2-17).","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t2-13"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"In the same testimony Osterholm predicted it was \"probable\" that human CWD cases from eating meat would be documented \"in the years ahead\", possibly many. About seven years on, none has been: CDC said in July 2026 that it has found no evidence of CWD in people (B2-13, not re-checked). This is a forecast that has not come true YET, not a refutation: the incubation could be 10+ years (B2-21).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-14"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"20 March 1996: Health Secretary Dorrell told Parliament that ten young people had a new variant of CJD, probably caught from BSE before the 1989 offal ban. The Edinburgh surveillance unit's paper (Will et al., Lancet, April 1996) described 10 cases with a new brain pathology, unusually young patients, and atypical EEGs. It said a BSE link was \"the most plausible explanation\" but could not yet be confirmed.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-15"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"UK vCJD count: 178 definite or probable cases to 31 Dec 2023 (123 definite, 55 probable), all dead. The last UK case was reported in 2016 (onset 2014). This matches the brief's comparator row (178, B2-21). Note: one line in the Volume 1 text reads \"over 801 cases\" by September 2000. That is a PDF artifact (80 plus footnote 1); the executive summary says \"over 80\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-16"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"Worldwide: 233 vCJD cases/deaths reported 1996–2023 (CDC). CIDRAP breaks it down as 178 UK plus 55 from 11 other countries, most of them people who had lived in the UK or in countries that imported UK feed or food. 4 were in the US, all believed infected abroad. The brief has no worldwide figure; add 233.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-17"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"vCJD struck the young: median age at death 28 (sporadic CJD: 68). The youngest UK case was 12 at onset.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-18"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"vCJD looked DIFFERENT, which is why it was spotted: psychiatric symptoms first, long illness, \"florid plaques\" in the brain, the \"pulvinar sign\" on MRI, and prions readily found in lymphoid tissue.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-19"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"Genetics: of 161 clinical UK cases with genetic analysis, 160 were methionine homozygous at PRNP codon 129 and one was MV. The unit warns that cases in other genotypes with longer incubation may still appear.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-20"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"Infection was far more common than disease: a survey of 32,441 archived UK appendixes found abnormal prion protein in 16, about 493 per million (roughly 1 in 2,000), against 177 clinical cases at the time. So the BSE \"attack rate\" in people was low, but silent carriage was not rare.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-21"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"Scale versus fear: a 2001 Science model estimated a mean incubation of 16.7 years and 205 total UK cases (upper limit 403). The real count (178) came in under that central estimate, and far below the fears of 1996. Useful as \"the warning was real, and the toll was smaller than feared\". (The upper bound and the model's assumptions are the authors'; no larger forecasts were checked here.)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-22"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"Oral exposure scale for BSE: about 1 million British cattle infected by the end of 1996, roughly half of them eaten before the end-1989 offal ban (the Royal Society, 1997, summarising Anderson et al., Nature 1996; the Anderson paper itself was not opened). Over 170,000 cattle were diagnosed. BSE peaked at \"thousands of cases a week\" of new infection.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-23"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"The often-quoted \"750,000 BSE-infected cattle entered the human food supply\" (Osterholm et al., mBio 2019) is the right order of magnitude but badly sourced: the paper's reference 7 for that number is a MeatEater podcast episode. Use T2-23 (about half a million before the 1989 ban, Royal Society/Anderson) instead. The same paper's \"nearly 1,000 cases confirmed each week at the peak\" is consistent with T2-23.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t2-24"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"CWD exposure scale: \"7,000 to 15,000 CWD-positive animals\" eaten per year, rising \"up to 20%\" a year. This is a ROUGH estimate by the Alliance for Public Wildlife (Geist et al. 2017, a \"Living Legacy White Paper\", advocacy and not peer-reviewed), relayed by Osterholm. The primary was not opened. No agency estimate was found, consistent with the dossier trap \"no source estimates how many people have eaten CWD-positive venison\". Don't air it as fact. If used at all: \"one advocacy group's rough guess\".","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-t2-25"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"BSE crossed easily into macaques by mouth: in Holznagel et al. (EID 2013), a single feed of 5 g of BSE cattle brain infected 18 of 18 macaques. That is the authors' count of infection found at any stage, including animals killed on schedule before symptoms. Single-dosed animals fell ill after a median 4.6 years. Lasmézas et al. (Lancet 2005) found one of two macaques fed 5 g developed vCJD-like disease at 60 months. CDC cites the 1996 macaque study (Lasmézas, Nature) as showing illness \"strikingly similar\" to vCJD; that study's route was not checked here.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-26"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"CWD at a comparable oral dose did NOT: NIH Rocky Mountain Labs fed macaques 4 g of CWD-infected brain in total (5 doses of 0.8 g) and found no clinical, pathological or biochemical transmission at 11–13 years (B2-15, not re-checked). EFSA (2019), weighing both macaque programmes: no classical prion disease in macaques 5–13 years after CWD challenge. Cross-check flag: EFSA describes the RML oral dose as 1 g against the Canadian 10 g, while Race 2018 itself says 4 g in total. Use Race's own figure.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-27"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"The caveat that keeps the precedent alive: the 2026 Calgary paper found low-level, transmissible prions in macaques (\"limited transmission\", B2-1 to B2-10 and B2-16). The authors say this shows the jump \"cannot be excluded\" and may hint at how a spillover would PRESENT. EFSA notes that BSE itself, under non-optimal exposure, produced an atypical disease in macaques that did not fit standard prion criteria. A human CWD case might not look like textbook prion disease.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-28"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"Where the prions sit, and where the precedent cuts the OTHER way. In BSE cattle the infectivity was concentrated in brain and spinal cord, and in 1997 it had not been detected in muscle (meat), which is why removing offal worked. In CWD deer, infectious prions are in skeletal muscle and in many tissues eaten as venison. So the \"just don't eat the brain\" logic that protected beef eaters does not carry over to deer.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-29"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"Strain biology: vCJD carries the BSE strain's fingerprint (identical strain-typing and western-blot glycoform patterns, unlike sporadic CJD). That is how the link was proved. CWD is not one strain but several, and CIDRAP warns some may cross species more easily, so \"past experience does not necessarily predict current or future risk\". This cuts both ways: no human case so far does not settle the question for every strain.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-30"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"vCJD was caught because the UK built a dedicated watch in May 1990 specifically to look for BSE's effect on CJD. The cases stood out by age and novel pathology.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-31"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"Would human CWD be recognisable? Maybe not quickly. CIDRAP: its brain pathology would be uncharacterised and might first be misfiled as sporadic or \"atypical\" CJD. CJD is not nationally notifiable in the US, and prion autopsy rates have fallen since 2019. (US surveillance \"may not be sensitive enough\" is B2-21.) CDC's own vCJD surveillance works by screening classic-CJD deaths for vCJD-like features such as young age; a CWD case need not share them.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-32"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"The time lag: vCJD surfaced about 10 years after BSE was recognised in cattle (1986 to 1996), and a CWD spillover could take \"years or even decades\" to show. CWD has been known since 1967, much longer than BSE had been before vCJD appeared, but it has spread widely only recently.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-33"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"The human record so far: studies in CWD-endemic areas show no rise in CJD or unusual prion disease. A 2018 Public Health Agency of Canada systematic review (23 studies) found no evidence of transmission to humans, but said it \"cannot be entirely ruled out\". Only BSE has been shown to jump from animals to people outside the lab.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-34"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"MEASURED, cited (B1-22). Testing uptake in Wisconsin, 2025 season: 17,759 deer sampled out of 339,481 registered, about 5.2% (B1's arithmetic). Testing is free. Low uptake is not a cost problem in Wisconsin.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-1"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"MEASURED, cited (B1-24). Minnesota 2025: about 14,000 samples, \"a little more than 7%\" of the harvest.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-2"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"SURVEYED (Virginia Department of Wildlife Resources; funded by USDA-APHIS; author Rene X. Valdez; mail and email, April-July 2022; 2,722 responses, 10.9% response rate, weighted). Only 7.8% of Virginia hunters had a deer tested in the past FIVE years. Trap: that is a five-year measure, not a season share, so never set it beside Wisconsin's 5% as like for like.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-3"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"SURVEYED, weak (an IDWeek 2024 poster abstract by Alabama College of Osteopathic Medicine students, published in Open Forum Infectious Diseases, Feb 2025; 125 hunters recruited through state organizations and social media, a convenience sample). The only number found on eating BEFORE results: 33.1% tested, and 58% of those testers did not wait for the result before eating. Never air it as a national rate.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-4"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"SURVEYED (North Carolina Wildlife Resources Commission, 2022 successful deer hunter survey; the fact sheet gives a ranked list with no percentages). Top five reasons for NOT testing: (1) CWD isn't in their area, (2) they didn't know how to submit a sample, (3) their deer looked healthy, (4) they aren't worried about eating a deer that might have CWD, (5) they aren't concerned about CWD. The agency notes reason 1 was given even INSIDE surveillance areas.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-5"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"SURVEYED (NCWRC 2022, same survey). Split by place: INSIDE the surveillance area the number-one reason for not testing was not being worried about eating a CWD-infected deer; OUTSIDE it, the belief that CWD isn't there.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-6"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"SURVEYED (NCWRC; willingness-to-pay sample March-August 2022, n = 1,029, 12% response). Over 95% said they would test or dispose safely if it were FREE. Shares rating each testing feature important: accuracy 93%, turnaround time 76%, convenience 74%, cost 71%.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-7"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"SURVEYED, weak (the same n = 125 convenience sample as T3-4). Barriers to testing: belief that CWD isn't in their area (44%) and no access to testing (39%). It points the same way as North Carolina's ranking.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-8"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"SURVEYED (Kentucky Dept. of Fish and Wildlife Resources, online, Oct-Dec 2022, hunters in or tied to the 5-county CWD surveillance zone; 1,694 responses, 25%). Among hunters who wanted more deer, 28.5% named too-long test turnaround as a barrier. Of those planning to hunt the zone again, 37.3% said they have \"no concerns about eating what I harvest\", and only 9.4% called the turnaround satisfactory.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-9"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"SURVEYED, the money side of testing (KDFWR 2022). Paying a processor for a deer that may come back positive is a cost. 26.8% said a free-processing voucher for a positive deer would make them take more deer. Asked directly, 82.2% said the risk of paying to process a deer that tests positive was \"not at all\" a barrier; 12.1% said somewhat, 5.8% very much. Money is a minority reason, not the main one. Wording note: this is about harvesting more deer, not about skipping a test.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-10"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"SURVEYED (Virginia DWR 2022). Asked about eating venison from a CWD county: if UNTESTED, 27.8% were not at all concerned, 53.5% slightly, 18.7% extremely; if it tested POSITIVE, 63.8% were extremely concerned. Behaviour: 93.5% reported no change in how much deer meat they eat, and 96.6% no change in where they hunt. This is the cleanest measured version of \"it hasn't changed what I do\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-11"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"SURVEYED (Virginia DWR 2022, hunters who had tested). Why they tested: \"help DWR monitor\" was rated very important by 68.4%, ahead of \"concern about eating meat\" at 35.9%. Even the testers were testing more for the herd than for their dinner.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-12"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"SURVEYED (Virginia DWR 2022). Meat is a big reason people hunt: 50.0% rated \"to obtain wild game meat\" very important, behind time in nature (80.9%) and family and friends (65.8%). In open-ended comments, hunters raised the long wait for results, and one family skipped a season while waiting on the previous year's results.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-13"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"SURVEYED, secondhand (Wisconsin DNR surveys of hunters whose deer tested positive, as reported by Isthmus, 2017-11-09; the DNR reports themselves were not found). 27% (2016-17) and 28% (2015-16) of hunters with a positive deer decided to eat it. The DNR itself warned that people change their minds both ways, and that \"we don't really know\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t3-14"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"DESCRIBED, conflicting. Outdoor Life (2024-07-24) quotes USGS's Bryan Richards: MOST Wisconsin respondents with positive deer said they planned to eat it. That contradicts the DNR's 27-28% (T3-14), and no survey report was found to settle it. Trap: never air \"most hunters eat positive deer\".","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-t3-15"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"SURVEYED, cited (B2-33). CDC drew on Wisconsin DNR hunter surveys run since 2003: 642 hunters with positive deer told surveyors they ate it OR intended to, and 3,940 more with positive deer (2020-22) were never asked. So most hunters with positive deer have no record of what they did with the meat.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-16"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"MEASURED, secondhand (Wisconsin DHS registry, via Isthmus 2017). 1,454 names on the registry of people known to have eaten infected venison, 2003-2015 harvests. That counts people, which can include family members, so it is not the 642 hunters. The registry was not read directly; B1 found the DHS pages returned 404.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t3-17"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"SURVEYED (Illinois; Vaske, Miller, Ashbrook, Needham, Human Dimensions of Wildlife 2018; mailed July-Nov 2012; 1,606 hunters in CWD counties, 1,958 in adjacent counties, 2,099 elsewhere; 52-58% response). The closest MEASURED thing to \"it's never been a problem\": hunters living with CWD perceived LESS risk to humans and to deer than hunters without it. The authors had predicted the opposite.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-18"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"DESCRIBED: the authors' explanation, NOT measured. Vaske et al. suggest that hunters in CWD counties, having seen more than 98% of tested deer come back negative, may use that plus anecdote to conclude the risk is low (personal experience). They also offer information overload and Illinois's low prevalence as possibilities. If the script uses \"it's never been a problem\", it must be framed as researchers' suggested explanation, not a finding.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-19"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"SURVEYED (Needham and Vaske 2008; surveys of 9,567 deer and elk hunters in eight states). Trust in the wildlife agency barely moves risk perception: hunters who trusted agencies perceived a little less risk, but trust explained at most 8% of it.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-20"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"SURVEYED, stated intentions only (Needham, Vaske, Manfredo 2004; a WAFWA project; 659 deer and elk hunters in eight states, hypothetical scenarios). At then-current prevalence, 3-5% said they would quit. At 50% prevalence, about 42% of residents and 54% of nonresidents would. With 50% prevalence and a hunter death, 68%. Trap: what people say about a hypothetical, not what they did.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-21"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"MEASURED, did risk change behaviour? Wisconsin gun-deer licence sales fell from 688,540 (2001) to 618,945 (2002), the year CWD was found. Bishop (UW-Madison staff paper 463, Nov 2003) calls it a decline of 75,767 or 10%; archery fell 19%, combined about 12%. CORRECTION of Bishop's own arithmetic: 688,540 - 618,945 = 69,595, not 75,767 (the 10% holds: 69,595 / 688,540 = 10.1%). Vaske et al. 2004 put the gun drop at about 11%. Air \"about one in ten\".","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-t3-22"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"SURVEYED (Vaske, Timmons, Beaman, Petchenik 2004; survey of 2001 Wisconsin resident deer hunters, n = 1,373, 67% response; abstract read). About half (52% ± 5%) of 2001 hunters who sat out 2002 did so because of CWD. Those CWD dropouts trusted the DNR less.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-23"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"SURVEYED, secondhand (an unpublished WI DNR survey by Petchenik, relayed by Bishop 2003). Outside the CWD counties, 12% of 2001 licence holders did not hunt in 2002, and 32% of those gave CWD as the reason: about a 4% drop. Bishop calls the licence data \"more solid\". The two Wisconsin estimates disagree, which shows the measurement is soft.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t3-24"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"DESCRIBED, secondhand (Responsive Management's Mark Damian Duda, via Outdoor Life 2024): when CWD turns up somewhere new, licence sales dip and then usually recover. Outdoor Life also reports Wisconsin lost about 60,000 hunters in 10 years, but state officials can't tie that to CWD. No recovery dataset was read.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t3-25"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"SURVEYED (Minnesota DNR with the MN Cooperative Fish and Wildlife Research Unit; southeast MN firearms hunters, fall-winter 2018-19; 2,086 returns, 42%). Concern is split almost evenly: on \"CWD will cause your family to stop eating deer meat\" (1 = not at all, 7 = extremely), 18.4% chose 1 and 14.0% chose 7. On disease in them personally, about 21% were at each extreme.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-26"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"SURVEYED (Wisconsin DNR social scientist Petchenik with CSU and OSU, J. Wildl. Manage. 2007; 613 landowners in the CWD eradication zone, 360 of them hunters). Landowners were on average concerned about the safety of eating venison. That concern is old, and it sits beside the low testing.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-27"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"MEASURED, cited (B1-21, B1-23, B1-25, B1-26). The practical gap: Wisconsin averaged 8.9 days from drop-off to result in 2025 (10-14 in an average year), Minnesota 7-14 days. Minnesota DNR says eating before results is the hunter's choice. A deer is usually butchered and frozen well inside that window.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-28"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"DESCRIBED, cited (B1-37, B1-39). The processor batch: Wisconsin warns that sausage and jerky may contain trim from other, untested deer, so a hunter who waits can still eat untested meat. Minnesota agriculture tells processors to hold carcass or trim until results. That is guidance, not a rule.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-29"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"DESCRIBED (interviews with 26 Michigan processors by MSU, MSU Extension, the Michigan DNR and UW-Madison, Human Dimensions of Wildlife; read through a Mining Journal write-up, Nov 2024). Processors wanted faster results and cheaper fees. Most had not changed their practices and saw compliance as the hunter's job. Processors who saw little risk reported little concern.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-30"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"SURVEYED (Goguen, Riley, Organ, Rudolph, Human Dimensions of Wildlife 2018, from the Michigan DNR's Deer Harvest Study, 2013 season; USGS abstract read). Venison as family food: 85% of successful hunters shared their venison, most often within their own household (69%). The 2013 haul was 11,402-14,473 metric tons of edible venison, about 25-32 million lb by my conversion.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-31"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"SURVEYED (Texas Wild Meat Sharing Survey, 2018, by Conservation Visions' Wild Harvest Initiative with Texas Parks and Wildlife; 2020 release). This was D43, UNRESOLVED. 97.7% of successful hunters share, reaching an average of 5.8 million people a year, more than half of them outside the hunter's household. D43's \"3.7 million outside\" is NOT in the release read here (only \"more than half\"). Air 97.7% and 5.8 million, attributed. Wild Harvest Initiative is a pro-hunting conservation group.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t3-32"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"SURVEYED, cited (D42, D36, D38). How many people eat it, and how much a deer gives: 75% of Michiganders have ever eaten game (59% of nonhunters); 11.5 million US big-game hunters (2022); an average Ohio deer gives 52.19 lb of venison. There is still no national count of households that eat venison (dossier gap 1 stands).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-33"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"DESCRIBED, cited (D30, D35, D36). The money: one Maine buck at about $3/lb after processing, against ground beef at $6.79/lb, saves about $160 a deer before gear (D35's arithmetic). A year's trips and gear ($1,352) spread over one deer is about $26/lb (D36). I found NO measured value-of-meat study for families, and did not run a dedicated search beyond the D rows. \"Money\" is only a sometime reason (T3-10).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-34"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"SURVEYED (two statewide representative surveys, Maine and Vermont, 2021-22, n = 2,001; Univ. of Vermont and Univ. of Maine; a medRxiv PREPRINT, not peer-reviewed). Food security: households that hunted ate wild game and red meat more often. Households doing most home and wild food activities had GREATER odds of being food insecure. That is an association: it can't say venison makes families food secure, only that hunting households include many struggling ones.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-35"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"DESCRIBED, a model read through a secondary summary (Hall, Gum, Pietkoski, Applied Geography 2020, West Virginia University; via a Public Health Post databyte). West Virginia hunters took over 60 million lb of game in 2012-17. IF pooled and shared out, it could cover the red-meat needs of over 35% of the state's food-insecure people. In practice it \"remains stored in hunters' freezers\". A what-if, not a measured outcome; the paper itself was not read.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t3-36"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"MEASURED, cited (D45, D44). Donations: Wisconsin's program has taken over 100,000 deer and made over 4 million lb of ground venison since 2000. NSSF (the firearms trade group) estimates about 1.06 million lb from six states in one year and says there is no national database.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-37"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"DESCRIBED (testimony by GLIFWC biologist Travis Bartnick to the House Appropriations Interior subcommittee, 2019-10-17; not a survey). For Ojibwe tribes in MN, WI and MI, deer is ceremonial food, part of tackling diabetes, and CWD is \"an existential threat\". The worry runs the OTHER way: that tribal hunters may stop eating a nutritious traditional food because they see it as unsafe. No tribal consumption or testing data was found.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-38"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"Familiarity or optimism bias, MEASURED? Not found. No study found measures optimism bias or \"it's never been a problem\" reasoning in hunters' food choices, for CWD or for lead. For lead, Fachehoun et al. 2015 (Quebec, random sample of hunters) \"documented the perception\" of hunters, but the abstract gives no perception figure. A \"41% say lead-shot meat is safe\" figure appeared only in a search summary and was not seen in any saved file. The nearest CWD evidence is indirect: T3-18 (less worry near CWD) and the North Carolina reasons in T3-5 (\"not in my area\", \"looked healthy\").","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-t3-39"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"Alberta 2009: \"37 per cent of responding hunters reported eating deer meat before provincial lab results came back\" (a University of Alberta master's study, per a Manitoba Co-operator story). Seen only in a search summary; the story returned Cloudflare 403 directly and through jina. Keep off air.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-t3-40"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"A measured value-of-venison study for low-income or rural households (dollars of meat a family gets, or how much of its protein comes from venison): none found. The MSU thesis that may hold Michigan household consumption figures (Goguen, d.lib.msu.edu etd 3785) returned HTTP 429.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-t3-41"},{"s":"ep50-is-deer-meat-good-for-you","n":50,"c":"Dossier and brief wording check: the brief's framing of the 642 as people \"known to have eaten\" positive venison. Already corrected by B2-33 (\"ate or intended to\"). It is repeated here because T3-14 to T3-16 together show the share of positive-deer hunters who EAT the meat is not known: 27-28% (DNR via Isthmus), \"most\" (Richards), and 3,940 never asked.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-t3-42"}]);